window.PLAYBOOK={"projects": [{"id": "MVE311TB-P1", "title": "Mini Graph-Based STA Engine Validated Against OpenSTA", "course": "MVE311TB", "course_title": "Static Timing Analysis with TCL Scripting", "sem": 1, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 22, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 1, "hay": "mve311tb-p1 mini graph-based sta engine validated against opensta static timing analysis with tcl scripting mve311tb students use sta as a black box, so slack numbers are never questioned. writing a small engine exposes how arrival times, required times and slew degradation are computed and where tool results can differ. parse a gate-level verilog netlist and a liberty subset (nldm tables with interpolation), build a timing graph, propagate arrival and slew in topological order, run backward required-time propagation, then report setup and hold slack. support create_clock, set_input_delay, set_output_delay. correlate every endpoint against opensta on the same netlist and library. netlist + liberty parser timing graph builder arrival/slew propagation required-time propagation slack report opensta correlation script timing graph algorithms, liberty modelling, delay calculation, tool correlation. units i, ii, iv, v: timing arcs, nldm delay tables, slew propagation, setup/hold, slack and a subset of sdc mve311ta-p1"}, {"id": "MVE311TB-P2", "title": "SDC and Tcl-Driven Timing Closure of an INT8 MAC Array", "course": "MVE311TB", "course_title": "Static Timing Analysis with TCL Scripting", "sem": 1, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 24, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 2, "hay": "mve311tb-p2 sdc and tcl-driven timing closure of an int8 mac array static timing analysis with tcl scripting mve311tb a systolic mac array is the core of every ai accelerator, and its critical path decides the clock. the question is how far constraints, pipelining and retiming can push frequency before area and power rise unacceptably. build a parameterised int8 mac array in verilog, write clock and i/o constraints, synthesise and place it with openroad, then iterate: inspect the worst paths, add pipeline stages, and apply multicycle or false-path exceptions only where they are architecturally justified. drive all analysis from opensta/openroad tcl scripts (path groups, filtered reports, a small tcl utility that ranks failing endpoints by cause). document each change and its slack effect. mac array rtl (parametric) sdc constraints yosys synthesis openroad place + sta tcl report scripts violation triage pipeline/retime changes timing closure workflow, constraint writing, tcl scripting for sta, microarchitecture timing trade-offs. units iv, v: sdc constraints and exceptions, tcl path tracing and report analysis, violation fixing; also practicals 10-11 (synthesis and sta) of mve212ia mve311ta-p2"}, {"id": "MVE311TB-P3", "title": "Slack Prediction from Pre-Route Features: A Variation-Aware Timing Study", "course": "MVE311TB", "course_title": "Static Timing Analysis with TCL Scripting", "sem": 1, "track": "C", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "8-12", "w0": 8, "total": 25, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 3, "hay": "mve311tb-p3 slack prediction from pre-route features: a variation-aware timing study static timing analysis with tcl scripting mve311tb late timing surprises cost design iterations. the hypothesis to test is whether features available before routing (fanout, estimated wire length, cell type, logic depth) can predict post-route slack well enough to guide early optimisation. generate a dataset by running many openroad flows over several open designs with varied constraints and utilisation. extract endpoint features pre-route and slack post-route. train simple models (linear, gradient-boosted trees) and compare to the estimate openroad already gives pre-route. report where models fail (crosstalk, long wires). design/constraint sweeper openroad flow runner feature extractor dataset store model training + eval error analysis data pipeline for eda, ml evaluation discipline, timing physics intuition. units ii, iii, v: parasitics, crosstalk, variability, post-route timing vs earlier estimates; tcl report extraction mve311ta-p3"}, {"id": "MVE212IA-P1", "title": "Multiplier Architecture Shootout for INT8 MAC Energy", "course": "MVE212IA", "course_title": "Low Power Digital IC Design", "sem": 1, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 22, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 4, "hay": "mve212ia-p1 multiplier architecture shootout for int8 mac energy low power digital ic design mve212ia the multiplier dominates mac energy. which int8 multiplier (array, baugh-wooley, radix-4 booth) is actually lowest energy per operation on realistic data, rather than random inputs? implement array, baugh-wooley and radix-4 booth multipliers with a shared accumulate stage. drive them with random data and with real quantised cnn activations and weights. estimate dynamic power from gate-level switching activity (vcd/saif) with opensta power analysis. three multiplier rtls common mac wrapper data generators (random, cnn) gate-level sim switching-activity power estimate comparison report arithmetic architecture, switching-activity power estimation. unit ii arithmetic building blocks and adders; practicals 6 (adder delay and power), 10 (synthesis with area, power and timing reports) and 12 (lec) mve212ia-p1"}, {"id": "MVE212IA-P2", "title": "Zero-Skipping and Clock-Gated PE Array: Measured Energy on Sparse Activations", "course": "MVE212IA", "course_title": "Low Power Digital IC Design", "sem": 1, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 25, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 5, "hay": "mve212ia-p2 zero-skipping and clock-gated pe array: measured energy on sparse activations low power digital ic design mve212ia post-relu activations are often sparse. gating idle processing elements should save dynamic power, but gating logic itself costs area and can hurt timing. build a baseline pe array and a variant with operand isolation, enable-based clock gating (using library icg cells) and zero detection. quantify energy per inference layer using real sparse feature maps from a small quantised cnn. pe (baseline) pe (gated) zero detector array controller activity-based power flow sparsity sweep power-aware microarchitecture, clock gating, power measurement discipline. units iii, v: signal gating, switching-activity reduction, architecture-level power; practicals 7 (master-slave dff), 9 (code coverage) and 10 (synthesis reports) mve212ia-p2"}, {"id": "MVE212IA-P3", "title": "Voltage-Parallelism-Frequency Trade-offs for an Accelerator: Calibrated Energy Model", "course": "MVE212IA", "course_title": "Low Power Digital IC Design", "sem": 1, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "8-10", "w0": 8, "total": 23, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 6, "hay": "mve212ia-p3 voltage-parallelism-frequency trade-offs for an accelerator: calibrated energy model low power digital ic design mve212ia doubling parallelism and lowering voltage can cut energy per operation, but leakage, memory and interconnect limit the gain. where is the optimum for a mac array under realistic constraints? characterise a mac cell across sky130 corners and supply voltages in ngspice, calibrate an analytical energy/delay model, and combine with a cycle-accurate array simulator to find energy-delay-optimal parallelism per layer. include a power-gating overhead model; state clearly that upf-driven power-gating verification is not fully supported by the open flow. spice characterisation calibrated energy/delay model array simulator optimiser sensitivity analysis quantitative power modelling, spice characterisation, design-space optimisation. unit v parallel architecture with voltage reduction, power and performance management; unit i power components; practical 5 (pvt variation) mve212ia-p3"}, {"id": "MVE213IA-P1", "title": "Boot-to-Driver Firmware Stack from Reset Vector", "course": "MVE213IA", "course_title": "Advanced Embedded System Design", "sem": 1, "track": "B", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 19, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 7, "hay": "mve213ia-p1 boot-to-driver firmware stack from reset vector advanced embedded system design mve213ia most students call vendor hal functions without understanding startup code, linker scripts and register-level drivers. firmware and driver roles require exactly that understanding. on a cortex-m or risc-v target (hardware or qemu/renode), write the reset handler, vector table, linker script, .data/.bss init, and register-level gpio, uart, systick and i2c drivers with unit tests that run on the emulator. startup + vector table linker script register-level drivers test harness (emulated) application bare-metal programming, linker scripts, driver development. unit iv linker scripts, elf, bootloaders, bsp and hal; unit iii memory-mapped i/o, uart and spi; experiments 1-4 and 6 mve313ia-p1"}, {"id": "MVE213IA-P2", "title": "RTOS INT8 Inference Runtime with DMA Double-Buffering", "course": "MVE213IA", "course_title": "Advanced Embedded System Design", "sem": 1, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 25, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 8, "hay": "mve213ia-p2 rtos int8 inference runtime with dma double-buffering advanced embedded system design mve213ia running neural-network inference on a microcontroller is a scheduling, memory and data-movement problem. the question is how much latency and cpu load a naive implementation wastes compared with a dma-overlapped design. run a small int8 cnn (keyword spotting or tiny image classifier) under freertos: sensor-capture task, dma double-buffer, inference task. compare naive c kernels with cmsis-nn style kernels and measure latency, cpu load, and deadline misses. sensor isr + dma double buffer inference task kernel library profiler (cycle counter) rtos design, dma, quantised inference runtime, profiling. unit v freertos, cmsis-rtos v2, tinyml and performance analysis; unit ii interrupt and dma controllers; experiments 7-13 mve313ia-p2"}, {"id": "MVE213IA-P3", "title": "Secure Boot Chain and Accelerator Driver for a RISC-V Platform", "course": "MVE213IA", "course_title": "Advanced Embedded System Design", "sem": 1, "track": "B", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "8-10", "w0": 8, "total": 22, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 9, "hay": "mve213ia-p3 secure boot chain and accelerator driver for a risc-v platform advanced embedded system design mve213ia firmware roles at accelerator and soc companies deal with trusted boot and device bring-up. the project asks how a minimal verified boot chain and a memory-mapped accelerator driver can be built and tested. implement stage-0 rom (hash + signature verification of stage-1), stage-1 loader, and an mpu/pmp configuration that protects accelerator registers. provide a driver for a simulated accelerator exposing csrs, dma descriptors and an interrupt. evaluate boot time and attack scenarios (corrupt image, rollback). rom stage 0 signature verification stage 1 loader pmp/mpu config accelerator driver fault/attack tests trusted boot concepts, pmp, driver design. unit iv bootloaders, secure boot, fota, elf and linker files; unit i risc-v architecture; unit iii memory-mapped i/o mve313ia-p3"}, {"id": "MVE314TA-P1", "title": "gm/ID Design Kit for Single-Stage and Differential Amplifiers: Hand Analysis vs ngspice in sky130", "course": "MVE314TA", "course_title": "Integrated Microelectronics Circuits Design", "sem": 1, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 23, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 10, "hay": "mve314ta-p1 gm/id design kit for single-stage and differential amplifiers: hand analysis vs ngspice in sky130 integrated microelectronics circuits design mve314ta hand analysis with square-law equations rarely matches a short-channel process, so students either distrust the maths or over-trust the simulator. the gm/id method connects the two with lookup tables generated from the actual pdk. sweep sky130 nfet and pfet devices in ngspice to build gm/id, intrinsic-gain, transit-frequency and current-density lookup tables. write a small python kit that sizes cs, cascode, source-follower and differential-pair stages from a spec (gain, bandwidth, load, current), predicts gain, swing and the dominant pole with hand-analysis formulas, then generates and runs the spice testbench and reports predicted vs simulated values. ngspice device sweeps gm/id lookup tables stage sizing scripts hand-analysis predictor auto-generated testbenches prediction-vs-simulation report small-signal analysis, gm/id sizing, spice automation, pdk device characterisation. units i-iii: mos small-signal model, cs stages with diode, current-source, active and triode loads and degeneration, source follower, common gate, cascode, differential pair, current mirrors; unit ii miller approximation "}, {"id": "MVE314TA-P2", "title": "Bandgap Reference and LDO for an Accelerator Rail", "course": "MVE314TA", "course_title": "Integrated Microelectronics Circuits Design", "sem": 1, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 23, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 11, "hay": "mve314ta-p2 bandgap reference and ldo for an accelerator rail integrated microelectronics circuits design mve314ta a mac array draws pulsed current. the regulator must keep droop within the digital timing budget while remaining stable. design a bandgap and a pmos ldo; drive the ldo with a load-current profile derived from the switching activity of your mac array (from mve212ia); evaluate psrr, load transient, loop stability across load. bandgap error amp pass device load profile stability analysis analog power delivery, mixed-domain analysis. unit iv bandgap references (ptat/ctat) and ldo regulators; unit iii current mirrors; also lab experiment 8 (bandgap) of mve221ia mve321ia-p2"}, {"id": "MVE314TA-P3", "title": "Sallen-Key, KHN and Tow-Thomas Biquads: Op-Amp Non-Idealities, Q Sensitivity and On-Chip Tuning", "course": "MVE314TA", "course_title": "Integrated Microelectronics Circuits Design", "sem": 1, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 23, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 12, "hay": "mve314ta-p3 sallen-key, khn and tow-thomas biquads: op-amp non-idealities, q sensitivity and on-chip tuning integrated microelectronics circuits design mve314ta active filters are designed with ideal op-amps on paper, but finite gain-bandwidth shifts the pole frequency and raises q, sometimes towards instability. the question is how much op-amp a given filter needs and which topology is least sensitive. design a second-order low-pass and band-pass filter (for example f0 = 100 khz, q = 5) in three topologies: sallen-key, khn and tow-thomas. derive the ideal transfer functions and q sensitivities to component values, then simulate each with a macro-model op-amp at swept gbw and with a transistor-level op-amp in sky130. add a capacitor-array tuning scheme and evaluate the monte-carlo spread of f0 and q before and after tuning. specification and ideal design symbolic sensitivity analysis macro-model op-amp sweep transistor-level op-amp monte-carlo tuning array filter synthesis, sensitivity analysis, op-amp and filter co-design, monte-carlo analysis. unit v: filter classification and transfer functions, second-order sections, sallen-key, khn and tow-thomas biquads, simulated-inductor biquads; units ii-iii for op-amp gain-bandwidth effects "}, {"id": "MVE315AA-P1", "title": "Parameterised INT8 MAC Array and GEMM Engine on FPGA", "course": "MVE315AA", "course_title": "Digital Systems Design and FPGA Implementation", "sem": 1, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 23, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 13, "hay": "mve315aa-p1 parameterised int8 mac array and gemm engine on fpga digital systems design and fpga implementation mve315aa fpga dsp and bram mapping decide performance. students rarely examine how rtl maps to resources. design a weight-stationary 4x4 to 16x16 mac array with bram tiles and a small controller; verify against numpy; synthesise and record resource use and fmax. pe array bram tile buffers fsm controller testbench + numpy golden fpga mapping, datapath design. units ii, iii: modelling styles, synthesis of datapaths and state machines; unit iv functional units for multiplication; unit v fpga acceleration for ml mve214a4-p1"}, {"id": "MVE315AA-P2", "title": "Streaming 3x3 Convolution Engine with Line Buffers", "course": "MVE315AA", "course_title": "Digital Systems Design and FPGA Implementation", "sem": 1, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 24, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 14, "hay": "mve315aa-p2 streaming 3x3 convolution engine with line buffers digital systems design and fpga implementation mve315aa streaming convolution minimises memory traffic by reuse via line buffers. correct boundary handling is subtle. build a pixel-stream 3x3 conv with bram line buffers, int8 weights, padding modes, backpressure. verify against opencv/numpy on images; measure throughput. line buffers window generator mac tree backpressure fsm reference streaming datapaths, handshakes. units iii, iv: synthesis and fpga fabric; unit v fpga acceleration for ml and edge ai mve214a4-p2"}, {"id": "MVE315AA-P3", "title": "RV32I 5-Stage Core from Scratch with riscv-formal Checks", "course": "MVE315AA", "course_title": "Digital Systems Design and FPGA Implementation", "sem": 1, "track": "C", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "8-12", "w0": 8, "total": 26, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 15, "hay": "mve315aa-p3 rv32i 5-stage core from scratch with riscv-formal checks digital systems design and fpga implementation mve315aa designing a pipelined cpu teaches hazards, forwarding, and verification at depth. the portfolio value is a verified core you control and can extend with custom instructions. implement rv32i with pipeline, forwarding, hazard detection, csr basics, and a memory interface; verify with the risc-v compliance tests and riscv-formal; run on fpga or simulation executing c programs. fetch decode execute memory writeback hazard unit formal harness cpu microarchitecture, formal verification. unit iv processor architecture design and stg-based controllers; units i-iii hdl modelling and synthesis mve214a4-p3"}, {"id": "MVE315AB-P1", "title": "GEMM Tiling Scheduler: DP and Search for Minimum DRAM Traffic", "course": "MVE315AB", "course_title": "AI Hardware Accelerators", "sem": 1, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "3-5", "w0": 3, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 16, "hay": "mve315ab-p1 gemm tiling scheduler: dp and search for minimum dram traffic ai hardware accelerators mve315ab mapping a matrix multiply onto an accelerator with limited scratchpad is a combinatorial problem, and unit ii of this course treats it as the core of dataflow and memory optimisation. naive tiling wastes dram bandwidth and energy. write a python tool that, given m,n,k, scratchpad size and loop order options, searches tile sizes and loop orders to minimise modelled dram traffic, and outputs a schedule consumable by later accelerator simulators. compare exhaustive search, greedy and dp/branch-and-bound. traffic model search engines schedule exporter validator algorithm design, reuse analysis, tooling. unit i tensor computation, memory hierarchy and energy; unit ii dataflow architectures and memory optimisation techniques mcs214a1-p1"}, {"id": "MVE315AB-P2", "title": "Quantisation Study: PTQ vs QAT from FP32 to INT4, with ONNX Runtime Benchmarks and RTL Weight Export", "course": "MVE315AB", "course_title": "AI Hardware Accelerators", "sem": 1, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 24, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 17, "hay": "mve315ab-p2 quantisation study: ptq vs qat from fp32 to int4, with onnx runtime benchmarks and rtl weight export ai hardware accelerators mve315ab accelerator design needs accuracy-vs-bit-width data from real networks. train a small cnn, apply post-training quantisation and quantisation-aware training at int8, int6 and int4, report accuracy and layer-wise sensitivity, benchmark fp32 and int8 models with onnx runtime on cpu (and on a gpu execution provider if available), and export weights and activation scales as hex for rtl tests. training ptq qat sensitivity analysis onnx export + benchmark rtl export tool quantisation methods. unit iii quantisation and mixed precision; unit iv onnx runtime and benchmarking methodology; unit v quantisation experiments (suggested exercise) mcs324c1-p1"}, {"id": "MVE315AB-P3", "title": "Layer-Fused Conv Accelerator Demo: End-to-End Tiny CNN in RTL", "course": "MVE315AB", "course_title": "AI Hardware Accelerators", "sem": 1, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "10-14", "w0": 10, "total": 27, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 18, "hay": "mve315ab-p3 layer-fused conv accelerator demo: end-to-end tiny cnn in rtl ai hardware accelerators mve315ab executing layer by layer spills activations to dram. layer fusion keeps them on-chip, but needs buffering and scheduling. implement a small cnn (for example mnist/cifar-scale) in rtl with and without fusion across two layers; measure memory traffic and cycles; verify against quantised pytorch. conv engine buffers fusion controller sw golden dataflow/fusion design. unit ii systolic and dataflow architectures; unit iii cnn acceleration and the suggested fpga-based nn acceleration exercise mcs324c1-p3"}, {"id": "MVE315AC-P1", "title": "Out-of-Order RISC-V Timing Model: Register Renaming, Issue Queue and ROB vs In-Order", "course": "MVE315AC", "course_title": "Advanced Computer Architecture with RISC-V", "sem": 1, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 25, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 19, "hay": "mve315ac-p1 out-of-order risc-v timing model: register renaming, issue queue and rob vs in-order advanced computer architecture with risc-v mve315ac out-of-order execution is usually taught with hand-drawn tables, so the cost and benefit of issue width, rob size and renaming are rarely measured. a trace-driven timing model shows how much ipc each structure buys on real risc-v programs. capture instruction traces from rv32im/rv64 programs with spike. build a cycle-level timing model in c++ or python that supports an in-order 5-stage baseline (i2o2) and an io2i machine with a rename table and free list, a unified issue queue, a reorder buffer with in-order commit and configurable branch prediction (bimodal, gshare). sweep widths and structure sizes and report ipc and structure occupancy. spike trace capture trace parser in-order baseline rename table + free list issue queue + rob branch predictor sweeps and plots out-of-order microarchitecture, register renaming, performance modelling, risc-v toolchain. units i-iii: pipelining and hazards, branch prediction, dynamic scheduling, i2o2 and io2i organisations, register renaming with pointers to the iq and rob "}, {"id": "MVE315AC-P2", "title": "RISC-V Vector (RVV) Subset Unit in RTL: Lane Scaling on DAXPY, GEMV and INT8 Dot Products", "course": "MVE315AC", "course_title": "Advanced Computer Architecture with RISC-V", "sem": 1, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "7-9", "w0": 7, "total": 26, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 20, "hay": "mve315ac-p2 risc-v vector (rvv) subset unit in rtl: lane scaling on daxpy, gemv and int8 dot products advanced computer architecture with risc-v mve315ac vector extensions are how risc-v cores compete on ml and dsp workloads, but the speed-up depends on lanes, vector length and memory bandwidth. this project measures those trade-offs on a real, verified unit. implement a decoupled vector coprocessor for a documented rvv 1.0 subset: vsetvli, unit-stride loads and stores, vadd, vmul, vmacc, vredsum and widening integer multiply-accumulate for int8 dot products. parameterise vlen and lane count, attach it to an open scalar core (or the rv32i core of mve315aa-p3) through a simple issue interface, verify it against spike, and benchmark kernels at different lane counts. scalar core interface vector decode + vsetvli banked vector register file lanes (alu, mac) load/store unit reduction tree spike co-simulation vector microarchitecture, rvv, co-simulation-based verification, ppa sweeps. unit iv: vector processors, vector parallelism, hardware optimisations (lanes, chaining), software and compiler optimisations, risc-v vector extension; unit i isa characteristics "}, {"id": "MVE315AC-P3", "title": "Trace-Driven Cache Simulator with Victim Cache, Prefetching and Tile-Aware Replacement", "course": "MVE315AC", "course_title": "Advanced Computer Architecture with RISC-V", "sem": 1, "track": "C", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 22, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 21, "hay": "mve315ac-p3 trace-driven cache simulator with victim cache, prefetching and tile-aware replacement advanced computer architecture with risc-v mve315ac unit v covers cache optimisations, victim caches and prefetching. accelerator and loop-nest access patterns are highly structured, so it is worth measuring how far classic policies are from the optimum and whether a schedule-aware policy helps. build a trace-driven cache simulator with lru, fifo, belady-opt, a victim cache, a stride prefetcher and a custom tile-aware replacement policy fed by the schedule from the gemm tiling scheduler (mve315ab-p1, or a simple loop-nest generator if you did not take that elective). generate traces from gemm, convolution and pointer-chasing loops and from risc-v programs run under spike, and evaluate hit rates and memory traffic. trace generator (loop nests + spike) cache model replacement policies victim cache stride prefetcher evaluator cache architecture, data structures, evaluation methodology. unit v cache performance, basic cache optimisations, victim caches and prefetching mcs214a1-p3"}, {"id": "MVE315AD-P1", "title": "1D Drift-Diffusion Solver for PN Junctions", "course": "MVE315AD", "course_title": "Semiconductor Device Modelling", "sem": 1, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 19, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 22, "hay": "mve315ad-p1 1d drift-diffusion solver for pn junctions semiconductor device modelling mve315ad black-box device simulators hide numerical issues. writing a solver builds depth. implement a 1d poisson + drift-diffusion solver (scharfetter-gummel) in python, validate against the ideal diode equation and depletion approximations, and explore doping and recombination effects. mesh poisson continuity solver validation numerical device modelling. units i, ii: poisson and continuity equations, drift-diffusion, p-n junction electrostatics and i-v mve323b3-p1"}, {"id": "MVE315AD-P2", "title": "EKV-Style Compact Model Extraction from sky130 SPICE Sweeps", "course": "MVE315AD", "course_title": "Semiconductor Device Modelling", "sem": 1, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 23, "hay": "mve315ad-p2 ekv-style compact model extraction from sky130 spice sweeps semiconductor device modelling mve315ad compact models drive circuit and architecture studies. a fitted model can generate fast delay/energy look-up tables. extract parameters of an ekv-like model from ngspice sweeps of sky130 devices, compare fit quality, and build gm/id tables used by the analog and low-power projects. sweep generator fitting validation lut export compact-model fitting. unit iv mosfet i-v, small-signal parameters, compact models and spice parameters; unit iii threshold voltage mve323b3-p2"}, {"id": "MVE315AD-P3", "title": "Cross-Layer Variability: Device Mismatch to Network Accuracy", "course": "MVE315AD", "course_title": "Semiconductor Device Modelling", "sem": 1, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "8-10", "w0": 8, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 24, "hay": "mve315ad-p3 cross-layer variability: device mismatch to network accuracy semiconductor device modelling mve315ad device variation shifts timing and analog values. how much matters for neural-network accuracy? take vth/mismatch distributions from monte-carlo spice, map to mac delay error or analog noise, inject in quantised network inference, and quantify accuracy vs variation level. mc spice error mapping inference injection mitigation study cross-layer reasoning. units iv, v: mosfet behaviour, short-channel effects and variability in scaled cmos mve323b3-p3"}, {"id": "MCS216TB-P1", "title": "LLM-Assisted RTL Generation Benchmark: Pass Rates Under Simulation and Formal Checks", "course": "MCS216TB", "course_title": "AI for Engineers", "sem": 1, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 25, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 25, "hay": "mcs216tb-p1 llm-assisted rtl generation benchmark: pass rates under simulation and formal checks ai for engineers mcs216tb engineers increasingly ask llms to write verilog, but plausible-looking rtl can be wrong in subtle ways. the question is how often generated rtl passes real checks, and which prompting strategies raise that rate. assemble 30 small hardware tasks (counters, fifos, arbiters, fsms, a crc unit, an axi-lite register file), each with a reference testbench and, where practical, symbiyosys properties. generate solutions with at least two models (a local open-weight model and, if available, a hosted one) under zero-shot, few-shot and self-repair prompting. run lint, simulation and formal checks automatically and report pass@k with failure categories. task set + specs reference testbenches prompt strategies generation harness lint/sim/formal runner results analysis llm prompting and evaluation, verification automation, rtl review. unit ii: foundation models, prompt engineering, zero- and few-shot prompting, ai-assisted programming and engineering applications; unit iii reliability and robustness "}, {"id": "MCS216TB-P2", "title": "Retrieval-Augmented Datasheet Assistant with Measured Retrieval and Answer Accuracy", "course": "MCS216TB", "course_title": "AI for Engineers", "sem": 1, "track": "B", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 23, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 26, "hay": "mcs216tb-p2 retrieval-augmented datasheet assistant with measured retrieval and answer accuracy ai for engineers mcs216tb engineers spend hours searching long reference manuals such as the stm32f407 rm0090, the axi specification or a pdk manual. a rag assistant can help, but only if its answers are grounded and its errors are measured. index 3-5 long technical pdfs (for example the stm32 reference manual, the cortex-m4 generic user guide, the risc-v privileged specification and the sky130 documentation). compare chunking strategies and two embedding models, store vectors in a local vector database, and answer questions with a local llm that must cite page numbers. build a 100-question test set with ground-truth answers and pages, and measure retrieval recall and answer correctness. pdf parser + chunker embedding models vector store retriever (+ reranker) llm with citations evaluation set + scorer rag pipelines, embeddings, evaluation design, handling technical documentation. unit iii: embeddings and similarity search, vector databases, retrieval-augmented generation, deployment, explainability and reliability; unit ii tokenisation and embeddings "}, {"id": "MCS216TB-P3", "title": "Tiny Transformer from Scratch: Attention, INT8 Inference and Accelerator Workload Counts", "course": "MCS216TB", "course_title": "AI for Engineers", "sem": 1, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 26, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 27, "hay": "mcs216tb-p3 tiny transformer from scratch: attention, int8 inference and accelerator workload counts ai for engineers mcs216tb transformer blocks dominate modern ai workloads, and hardware designers need to know where the operations and memory traffic go. building one from scratch shows exactly which matrix multiplies and softmax steps a chip has to accelerate. implement a decoder-only transformer (2-4 layers, d_model 128) in numpy with forward and backward passes, check gradients against pytorch, and train it on a character-level dataset. then write an int8 inference path (per-channel weight quantisation, integer gemms with requantisation, fixed-point softmax and layernorm approximations) and measure the accuracy loss. count macs, memory traffic and kv-cache size per token and relate them to an accelerator's compute and bandwidth. numpy layers gradient check vs pytorch training loop int8 inference path fixed-point softmax/layernorm operation and traffic counter transformer internals, numerical verification, quantisation, workload characterisation. units i-ii: neural networks and backpropagation, attention, self- and multi-head attention, positional encoding, transformer architecture; unit iii edge deployment and robustness "}, {"id": "HSS217TA-P1", "title": "Patent Landscape for Systolic and Dataflow AI Accelerators", "course": "HSS217TA", "course_title": "Intellectual Property Rights", "sem": 1, "track": "A", "diff": "Beginner", "label": "Build first", "wk": "3-4", "w0": 3, "total": 19, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 28, "hay": "hss217ta-p1 patent landscape for systolic and dataflow ai accelerators intellectual property rights hss217ta accelerator dataflows are heavily patented. before building a product or publishing, an engineer should know how to search patents, read claims and see where a design might overlap. search patent databases for systolic-array and dataflow-accelerator patents, classify 30-50 relevant ones by technique (weight-stationary, output-stationary, sparsity, quantisation), read the independent claims of the five most cited, and compare them at a conceptual level with the mac array you build in mve315aa-p1. produce a landscape chart and plain-language claim summaries, clearly labelled as a learning exercise and not legal advice. search strategy (keywords, cpc codes) patent set classification claim reading landscape chart overlap notes patent search and claim reading, technical landscaping, ip awareness. unit i: scope of patents, patentable inventions, patent search, infringement, commercialisation of ip "}, {"id": "HSS217TA-P2", "title": "Open-Source Hardware Licence Audit and SBOM for Your Project Stack", "course": "HSS217TA", "course_title": "Intellectual Property Rights", "sem": 1, "track": "C", "diff": "Beginner", "label": "Build next", "wk": "2-3", "w0": 2, "total": 18, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 29, "hay": "hss217ta-p2 open-source hardware licence audit and sbom for your project stack intellectual property rights hss217ta student projects combine open cores, pdks, scripts, python packages, datasets and model weights under different licences (apache-2.0, solderpad, cern-ohl, gpl, bsd, non-commercial dataset terms). mixing them carelessly can make a project impossible to publish or commercialise. inventory every third-party component in two of your projects, identify each licence and its obligations (attribution, notices, share-alike, patent grant), check compatibility, and write a notice file and a licence policy for your repository. generate an spdx bill of materials automatically and check it in ci. component inventory licence identification compatibility matrix notice and policy spdx sbom ci check open-source compliance, copyright, sbom tooling. unit iii: copyright, nature and scope, rights conferred, transfer and exceptions, infringement; unit i types of ip "}, {"id": "HSS217TA-P3", "title": "Invention Disclosure and Provisional-Style Patent Draft for One of Your Own Ideas", "course": "HSS217TA", "course_title": "Intellectual Property Rights", "sem": 1, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "3-4", "w0": 3, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 30, "hay": "hss217ta-p3 invention disclosure and provisional-style patent draft for one of your own ideas intellectual property rights hss217ta minor and major projects produce new ideas, but students rarely know whether to publish, patent or keep them as trade secrets. writing a disclosure forces a precise statement of what is actually new. pick one idea from your own work (for example a dataflow trick, a test-compression scheme or a calibration method). write an invention disclosure, run a prior-art search, draft claims and a provisional-style specification with figures, and write a decision memo comparing patenting, publishing and trade-secret protection, with rough indian costs and timelines. idea selection prior-art search disclosure form claims draft specification + figures decision memo claim drafting, prior-art analysis, ip strategy. unit i: patent procedure, patent drafting, transfer of rights, commercialisation and valuation; unit iii trade secrets as an alternative "}, {"id": "HSS116EL-P1", "title": "Architecture Specification for an Accelerator (Design-Spec Document)", "course": "HSS116EL", "course_title": "Technical English (Online)", "sem": 1, "track": "A", "diff": "Beginner", "label": "Build first", "wk": "2-3", "w0": 2, "total": 17, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 31, "hay": "hss116el-p1 architecture specification for an accelerator (design-spec document) technical english (online) hss116el weak specifications cause rework. the project writes the kind of spec used inside chip teams. write a 12-15 page architecture spec for the mac-array accelerator: goals, interfaces, register map, timing diagrams, verification plan. peer review with a checklist. template draft peer review revision technical writing. unit i business and technical documents; unit ii recommendations; unit iii audience needs hss116el-p1"}, {"id": "HSS116EL-P2", "title": "Evidence-Based White Paper: INT8 vs FP16 Inference Trade-offs", "course": "HSS116EL", "course_title": "Technical English (Online)", "sem": 1, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "3-4", "w0": 3, "total": 19, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 32, "hay": "hss116el-p2 evidence-based white paper: int8 vs fp16 inference trade-offs technical english (online) hss116el marketing claims about precision are often misleading. a white paper must use sources and data honestly. using your own measurements (quantisation experiments) and vetted sources, write a 10-page white paper with recommendations and limits; include a source-quality table. literature data analysis draft review evidence-based communication. unit ii interpreting data, ethical persuasion, recommendations; unit iii trustworthy sources hss116el-p2"}, {"id": "HSS116EL-P3", "title": "Conference-Style Paper and Talk Replicating a Published Roofline Study", "course": "HSS116EL", "course_title": "Technical English (Online)", "sem": 1, "track": "B", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "5-6", "w0": 5, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 33, "hay": "hss116el-p3 conference-style paper and talk replicating a published roofline study technical english (online) hss116el reproducing a published analysis teaches research communication and honest comparison. replicate a roofline analysis (williams et al., cacm 2009) for gemm on your cpu/gpu, write an ieee-format 4-page paper with reproducibility artefacts, and give a 10-minute talk; also peer-review two papers. replication paper talk peer review research communication. unit iii genres, standards vs white papers; unit ii calls for proposals; presentation skills hss116el-p3"}, {"id": "MVE221IA-P1", "title": "Two-Stage Miller Op-Amp: Spec to Post-Layout in sky130", "course": "MVE221IA", "course_title": "Analog and Mixed-signal IC Design", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 22, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 34, "hay": "mve221ia-p1 two-stage miller op-amp: spec to post-layout in sky130 analog and mixed-signal ic design mve221ia hand-calculated sizing rarely survives simulation, and layout parasitics erode phase margin. the project closes the loop from specification to post-layout. size a two-stage miller-compensated op-amp from specs (gain, gbw, pm, power), simulate dc/ac/transient/corners in ngspice, draw layout in magic or klayout with matching care, extract parasitics, and compare pre- and post-layout. spec sheet hand sizing (gm/id) ngspice testbenches layout pex corner report analog design flow, compensation, layout parasitics. unit i two-stage op-amps and psrr; unit ii stability, pole splitting and miller compensation; lab experiments 6-7 mve321ia-p1"}, {"id": "MVE221IA-P2", "title": "Switched-Capacitor Integrator and Bottom-Plate Sample-and-Hold: Charge Injection and kT/C in sky130", "course": "MVE221IA", "course_title": "Analog and Mixed-signal IC Design", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 23, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 35, "hay": "mve221ia-p2 switched-capacitor integrator and bottom-plate sample-and-hold: charge injection and kt/c in sky130 analog and mixed-signal ic design mve221ia switched-capacitor circuits are the front end of almost every adc, but their errors (charge injection, clock feedthrough, kt/c noise, signal-dependent switch resistance) are easy to mis-estimate. this project measures each error in simulation and checks it against theory. design an nmos switch, a cmos transmission gate, a bootstrapped switch and a bottom-plate sample-and-hold in sky130. measure the charge-injection pedestal, distortion (thd and sfdr from a coherent fft) and noise against analytical models. then build a parasitic-insensitive sc integrator around a simple ota and measure gain error and settling vs op-amp gain and bandwidth. switch variants bottom-plate s/h non-overlapping clock generator transient + fft scripts noise analysis sc integrator with ota sampling circuits, sc design, coherent fft testing, noise analysis. unit iii: mosfets as sampling switches, switch distortion, channel charge injection, clock feedthrough, bottom-plate sampling, parasitic-insensitive sc integrator, sample-and-hold characteristics "}, {"id": "MVE221IA-P3", "title": "Transistor-Level 8-bit SAR ADC in sky130 with FFT-Based ENOB and Monte-Carlo Linearity", "course": "MVE221IA", "course_title": "Analog and Mixed-signal IC Design", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "8-10", "w0": 8, "total": 25, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 36, "hay": "mve221ia-p3 transistor-level 8-bit sar adc in sky130 with fft-based enob and monte-carlo linearity analog and mixed-signal ic design mve221ia sar adcs are the standard choice for sensor and low-power accelerator readout, and the lab asks for one. moving from a behavioural model to transistor level exposes comparator offset, capacitor mismatch and dac settling, which decide the real enob. design an 8-bit charge-redistribution sar adc in sky130: a binary-weighted capacitor dac (optionally split), a latch comparator with a preamp, a bootstrapped sampling switch and sar logic synthesised from verilog. simulate at 1 ms/s, compute enob, sfdr, inl and dnl from coherent fft and ramp tests, and run monte-carlo on capacitor mismatch and comparator offset. bootstrapped s/h capacitor dac preamp + latch comparator sar logic mixed-signal testbench fft + ramp analysis monte-carlo mixed-signal design, comparator and dac design, data-converter characterisation. units iii-iv: sample-and-hold, data-converter specifications, charge-scaling dac, successive-approximation adc; lab experiment 10 (sar adc design and verification) "}, {"id": "MVE222IA-P1", "title": "Constrained-Random Class-Based Testbench for an AXI-Stream FIFO", "course": "MVE222IA", "course_title": "Systemverilog for Verification", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 37, "hay": "mve222ia-p1 constrained-random class-based testbench for an axi-stream fifo systemverilog for verification mve222ia directed tests miss corner cases. a class-based random testbench with scoreboard and coverage shows verification fundamentals. build generator, driver, monitor, scoreboard and coverage groups for an axi-stream fifo with sva protocol checks. seed bugs to confirm the bench catches them. interface generator driver monitor scoreboard coverage + sva sv verification methodology. units ii, iii, v: classes, randomisation, interfaces and clocking blocks, covergroups mve323b1-p1"}, {"id": "MVE222IA-P2", "title": "AXI4-Lite / AXI-Stream Verification IP and Protocol Assertions for Accelerator CSRs", "course": "MVE222IA", "course_title": "Systemverilog for Verification", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 23, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 38, "hay": "mve222ia-p2 axi4-lite / axi-stream verification ip and protocol assertions for accelerator csrs systemverilog for verification mve222ia soc integration bugs hide at interfaces. a reusable bus agent with checkers is core industrial verification work. build axi4-lite master/slave agents, a register model, sva for protocol rules (valid/ready stability, response rules), and run against your accelerator csr block; report coverage closure. axi-lite agent register model sva checker test sequences coverage bus protocols, vip design. units iii, iv, v: interfaces, scoreboards, concurrent assertions and formal property checks mve323b1-p2"}, {"id": "MVE222IA-P3", "title": "Mutation-Tested Verification of a Systolic GEMM Engine", "course": "MVE222IA", "course_title": "Systemverilog for Verification", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "8-10", "w0": 8, "total": 25, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 39, "hay": "mve222ia-p3 mutation-tested verification of a systolic gemm engine systemverilog for verification mve222ia coverage does not prove a testbench finds bugs. mutation analysis estimates bug-finding power. build a verification environment for the gemm engine with a reference model; automatically inject mutants into the rtl; measure kill rate versus coverage and compare constrained-random against directed and formal checks. reference model environment mutator kill-rate analysis verification quality metrics. units iv, v: verification metrics, coverage and assertions, functional vs formal; methodology evaluation mve323b1-p3"}, {"id": "MVE223TA-P1", "title": "Stuck-At Fault Simulator and PODEM ATPG on ISCAS Circuits", "course": "MVE223TA", "course_title": "VLSI Testing", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 22, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 40, "hay": "mve223ta-p1 stuck-at fault simulator and podem atpg on iscas circuits vlsi testing mve223ta understanding test generation internals clarifies coverage. implement a parallel-pattern fault simulator and podem for combinational iscas circuits; report coverage vs patterns. netlist parser fault list fault sim podem atpg. unit ii podem, path sensitisation and fault simulation; unit i fault equivalence and collapsing mve232e1-p1"}, {"id": "MVE223TA-P2", "title": "Scan, LFSR/MISR BIST Insertion and Fault Coverage for a MAC Array", "course": "MVE223TA", "course_title": "VLSI Testing", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 24, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 41, "hay": "mve223ta-p2 scan, lfsr/misr bist insertion and fault coverage for a mac array vlsi testing mve223ta testing accelerators needs scan and bist schemes with acceptable overhead. add scan chains (using an open dft flow or own scripts) and logic bist (lfsr/misr) to a mac array; evaluate fault coverage vs pattern count and area overhead. scan insertion lfsr misr fault sim dft. units iii, iv: scan design rules, logic bist with lfsr and misr, fault coverage mve232e1-p2"}, {"id": "MVE223TA-P3", "title": "March-Test Memory BIST for Accelerator Scratchpad with Fault Injection", "course": "MVE223TA", "course_title": "VLSI Testing", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 24, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 42, "hay": "mve223ta-p3 march-test memory bist for accelerator scratchpad with fault injection vlsi testing mve223ta scratchpads dominate accelerator area; memory defects dominate yield. implement a march c- bist controller in rtl for a scratchpad, with fault models (saf, tf, coupling) injected in simulation; evaluate coverage and overhead; explore testing pe-array structures using column-wise patterns. memory model with faults march fsm coverage analysis memory test. unit v memory fault models, march tests and mbist architecture mve232e1-p3"}, {"id": "MVE224BA-P1", "title": "Partitioning and Simulated-Annealing Floorplanner on Public Benchmarks", "course": "MVE224BA", "course_title": "VLSI Physical Design and Algorithms", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 43, "hay": "mve224ba-p1 partitioning and simulated-annealing floorplanner on public benchmarks vlsi physical design and algorithms mve224ba understanding sa and group migration algorithms clarifies how tools trade runtime and quality. implement kl/fm partitioning and an sa floorplanner (sequence-pair or b*-tree) on mcnc/gsrc-style benchmarks; compare to random and report area and wirelength. benchmark parser fm sa floorplanner metrics eda algorithms. unit ii partitioning (group migration such as kl/fm, simulated annealing); unit iii floorplanning mve331ta-p1"}, {"id": "MVE224BA-P2", "title": "Systolic Array Physical-Design Exploration: Aspect Ratio, Macros and Power Grid", "course": "MVE224BA", "course_title": "VLSI Physical Design and Algorithms", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 25, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 44, "hay": "mve224ba-p2 systolic array physical-design exploration: aspect ratio, macros and power grid vlsi physical design and algorithms mve224ba regular arrays are wire-dominated. floorplan choices change wirelength, congestion and timing. sweep array size, aspect ratio, utilisation, pdn pitch, and sram macro placement for a 16x16 array with scratchpads; report ppa pareto and ir drop. parameter sweeper flow runner metrics db pareto plots floorplanning, ppa analysis. unit ii design planning: macro placement, power and clock planning; unit v power analysis mve322ia-p2"}, {"id": "MVE224BA-P3", "title": "GPU-Accelerated Analytic Placement Kernels (HPWL and Density) in CUDA", "course": "MVE224BA", "course_title": "VLSI Physical Design and Algorithms", "sem": 2, "track": "B", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "8-12", "w0": 8, "total": 27, "cuda": true, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 45, "hay": "mve224ba-p3 gpu-accelerated analytic placement kernels (hpwl and density) in cuda vlsi physical design and algorithms mve224ba placement is a large optimisation. gpu acceleration is established (for example dreamplace, lin et al., dac 2019), so the contribution here is a learning implementation and kernel study. implement a simple analytic placer on cpu (weighted-average wirelength plus density), then cuda kernels for wirelength gradient and density smoothing; profile and measure speedup and solution quality. cpu placer wa wirelength kernel density kernel optimiser profiler gpu kernels for irregular problems, eda. unit iii global placement; unit ii graph data structures; unit v machine learning in physical design (context) mve331ta-p3"}, {"id": "MVE224BB-P1", "title": "RF Cascade and Matching-Network Design Tool", "course": "MVE224BB", "course_title": "RFIC Design", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "3-5", "w0": 3, "total": 17, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 46, "hay": "mve224bb-p1 rf cascade and matching-network design tool rfic design mve224bb hand calculation of cascades and l-match networks is error-prone. a validated tool builds intuition. python tool using scikit-rf for cascaded nf/iip3/gain, l and pi matching, and smith-chart plots, validated against ngspice ac analysis of the same networks. cascade calculator matching designer ngspice validation rf system analysis. units i, ii: noise figure of cascades, iip3, smith chart and impedance matching mve323b2-p1"}, {"id": "MVE224BB-P2", "title": "Ring/LC Oscillator Phase-Noise Study", "course": "MVE224BB", "course_title": "RFIC Design", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 47, "hay": "mve224bb-p2 ring/lc oscillator phase-noise study rfic design mve224bb oscillator noise limits clock quality. ring oscillators are common in digital socs; their jitter matters for timing. design a ring oscillator and an lc oscillator in ngspice, estimate phase noise via transient noise and fft, compare against analytical models (leeson/hajimiri) and relate jitter to an sta uncertainty budget. ring oscillator lc oscillator noise sim jitter conversion oscillator/jitter analysis. unit iv lc oscillators and phase noise; unit ii device noise mve323b2-p2"}, {"id": "MVE224BB-P3", "title": "Digital Down-Converter RTL: CIC, FIR and I/Q Correction", "course": "MVE224BB", "course_title": "RFIC Design", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "6-9", "w0": 6, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 48, "hay": "mve224bb-p3 digital down-converter rtl: cic, fir and i/q correction rfic design mve224bb modern receivers shift work into digital. a ddc is a streaming-dsp datapath with real throughput and precision trade-offs. implement nco, mixer, cic decimator, compensating fir and i/q imbalance correction in rtl; verify in python; evaluate snr, resource use, and bit-width choices. nco mixer cic fir iq correction python model dsp datapath design. units i, v: receiver architectures and software-defined radio mve323b2-p3"}, {"id": "MVE224BC-P1", "title": "OpenMP and MPI Scaling Study of a Stencil Code: Measured Speed-Up vs Amdahl and Gustafson", "course": "MVE224BC", "course_title": "High Performance Embedded Computing and GPU Architecture", "sem": 2, "track": "B", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 22, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 49, "hay": "mve224bc-p1 openmp and mpi scaling study of a stencil code: measured speed-up vs amdahl and gustafson high performance embedded computing and gpu architecture mve224bc parallel speed-up is often reported without a model of why it stops. a controlled study on one kernel shows serial fractions, memory-bandwidth limits and communication costs in real numbers. implement a 2-d heat-equation (5-point stencil) solver in c in three versions: openmp, mpi with halo exchange, and hybrid mpi+openmp. measure strong and weak scaling on a multi-core workstation (and on a small cluster or the college hpc if available), fit amdahl and gustafson models, measure memory bandwidth with stream, and explain where scaling departs from the models. serial reference openmp version mpi halo exchange hybrid version benchmark harness stream + roofline model fitting openmp, mpi, performance measurement, scaling analysis, roofline. unit i: flynn taxonomy, shared and distributed memory, speedup, efficiency, amdahl and gustafson laws; unit ii openmp, mpi and hybrid mpi+openmp; unit iii clusters and energy-efficient computing "}, {"id": "MVE224BC-P2", "title": "CUDA Image-Processing Pipeline: Shared-Memory Tiling, Coalescing, Bank Conflicts and Streams", "course": "MVE224BC", "course_title": "High Performance Embedded Computing and GPU Architecture", "sem": 2, "track": "B", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 20, "cuda": true, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 50, "hay": "mve224bc-p2 cuda image-processing pipeline: shared-memory tiling, coalescing, bank conflicts and streams high performance embedded computing and gpu architecture mve224bc image filters are bandwidth bound, which makes them the cleanest case study for unit iv: memory spaces, coalescing, bank conflicts, occupancy and stream overlap all show up directly in the numbers. write cuda kernels for separable gaussian, sobel and harris corner detection. build an optimisation ladder (naive global memory, coalesced, shared-memory tiles with halos, constant-memory coefficients, padded shared memory to remove bank conflicts, kernel fusion) and then overlap transfers and compute across cuda streams for a video sequence. profile every step with nsight compute and nsight systems and compare with opencv on the cpu. naive kernels coalesced + tiled kernels bank-conflict-free shared memory fused kernel multi-stream pipeline nsight analysis cuda optimisation. unit iv gpu memory optimisation, coalescing, bank conflicts, streams and nsight profiling; image-processing case study mcs324c1-p2"}, {"id": "MVE224BC-P3", "title": "Host-GPU Data Path Study: Pageable, Pinned and Unified Memory, Streams and Peer-to-Peer Copies", "course": "MVE224BC", "course_title": "High Performance Embedded Computing and GPU Architecture", "sem": 2, "track": "B", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 24, "cuda": true, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 51, "hay": "mve224bc-p3 host-gpu data path study: pageable, pinned and unified memory, streams and peer-to-peer copies high performance embedded computing and gpu architecture mve224bc for many real workloads the gpu waits on data, not on compute. how much bandwidth and latency each host-memory mode delivers, and how well copies overlap with kernels, decides the end-to-end speed-up. write a micro-benchmark suite that measures host-to-device and device-to-host bandwidth and latency for pageable, pinned and mapped (zero-copy) memory, and the page-fault behaviour of unified memory with and without prefetch hints. then build a streamed pipeline (copy in, kernel, copy out across n streams) for a realistic kernel and measure overlap. with two gpus, measure peer-to-peer copies; otherwise document the pcie topology and expected limits. bandwidth/latency micro-benchmarks unified-memory fault study streamed pipeline nsight systems timelines pcie topology report model vs measurement gpu memory systems, dma and pcie, cuda streams, profiling. unit v: i/o subsystem, pci interfacing, dma, iommu, peer-to-peer dma, virtual and unified virtual memory, gpu drivers; unit iv asynchronous execution, cuda streams, multi-gpu concepts "}, {"id": "MVE224BD-P1", "title": "Dual-Bank OTA Update with Signed Images, Rollback and a Virtual-ECU CI Pipeline", "course": "MVE224BD", "course_title": "Software Defined Vehicles", "sem": 2, "track": "B", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 23, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 52, "hay": "mve224bd-p1 dual-bank ota update with signed images, rollback and a virtual-ecu ci pipeline software defined vehicles mve224bd software-defined vehicles depend on updates that never brick an ecu. the engineering questions are how to verify image authenticity, survive power loss mid-update, and test all of it automatically before any hardware is touched. on an stm32f4 board (or a renode-emulated equivalent) implement a bootloader with two application slots, signed images (ed25519 or ecdsa p-256), a version counter for anti-rollback and a confirm-or-revert flow after boot. run the same firmware on a renode virtual ecu in ci with update scenarios (good image, corrupted image, wrong signature, power loss at random points) and publish a pass/fail matrix. bootloader slot manager signature verification anti-rollback counter update agent (uart/can) renode virtual ecu ci fault scenarios secure update design, embedded bootloaders, ci with virtual hardware, automotive cybersecurity basics. unit iii: fota, dual-bank flashing and rollback strategies; unit iv secure boot, pki and hsm concepts; unit v automotive ci/cd, containers and virtual ecus "}, {"id": "MVE224BD-P2", "title": "Zonal Gateway in RTL: CAN-FD to Automotive Ethernet Bridge with Deterministic Latency", "course": "MVE224BD", "course_title": "Software Defined Vehicles", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Build next", "wk": "8-10", "w0": 8, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 53, "hay": "mve224bd-p2 zonal gateway in rtl: can-fd to automotive ethernet bridge with deterministic latency software defined vehicles mve224bd zonal e/e architectures concentrate many can-fd and lin buses into zone controllers that forward traffic over automotive ethernet. the gateway must translate frames and guarantee bounded latency for safety-relevant messages. implement a gateway datapath in rtl: can-fd frame parser (from a bit-level model or a controller interface), id-based filter and routing table, priority queues, an ethernet frame builder over axi-stream, and a latency monitor. verify worst-case latency under synthetic and recorded load. can-fd frame interface filter + routing table priority scheduler ethernet frame builder latency monitor network hardware. unit i zonal architecture, can/can-fd and automotive ethernet; unit iv fail-operational design met325dj-p3"}, {"id": "MVE224BD-P3", "title": "Soft-Error Fault Injection and Mitigation for a Systolic Array (ISO 26262 Concepts)", "course": "MVE224BD", "course_title": "Software Defined Vehicles", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "8-12", "w0": 8, "total": 26, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 54, "hay": "mve224bd-p3 soft-error fault injection and mitigation for a systolic array (iso 26262 concepts) software defined vehicles mve224bd automotive chips need quantified resilience for their asil target. soft errors in mac arrays used for perception can silently corrupt results, which is exactly the kind of fault iso 26262 metrics are meant to expose. inject bit flips into registers of the systolic array rtl via simulation; classify outcomes (masked, sdc, due); evaluate parity, ecc and selective tmr for area overhead versus coverage. fault injector outcome classifier mitigation rtl area model safety analysis, fault injection. unit iv iso 26262 functional safety, asil levels and fail-operational design mve323b4-p3"}, {"id": "MBT325DA-P1", "title": "Evolutionary Design-Space Exploration of Accelerator Configurations", "course": "MBT325DA", "course_title": "Nature Impelled Engineering", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 22, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 55, "hay": "mbt325da-p1 evolutionary design-space exploration of accelerator configurations nature impelled engineering mbt325da accelerator parameters (pe count, sram size, dataflow) interact non-linearly. bio-inspired search can explore them efficiently. build an analytical accelerator model (latency, energy, area) and search it with ga, pso and random search; compare convergence and pareto quality. analytical model ga pso random baseline pareto analysis optimisation, dse. unit v genetic algorithms, evolutionary computing and swarm intelligence mbt325da-p1"}, {"id": "MBT325DA-P2", "title": "Spiking LIF Neuron Array in RTL: Event-Driven vs Dense Energy", "course": "MBT325DA", "course_title": "Nature Impelled Engineering", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 56, "hay": "mbt325da-p2 spiking lif neuron array in rtl: event-driven vs dense energy nature impelled engineering mbt325da spiking networks promise energy savings through sparsity. the claim must be tested against a dense baseline on the same task. implement leaky-integrate-and-fire neuron arrays with event queues, run a small classification task, and compare synaptic operations and estimated energy against a quantised dense network. lif neuron event queue synapse memory dense baseline neuromorphic architecture. unit v neural networks and bio-inspired computing mbt325da-p2"}, {"id": "MBT325DA-P3", "title": "Self-Repairing PE Array: Spare-Row/Column Reconfiguration", "course": "MBT325DA", "course_title": "Nature Impelled Engineering", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "7-9", "w0": 7, "total": 23, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 57, "hay": "mbt325da-p3 self-repairing pe array: spare-row/column reconfiguration nature impelled engineering mbt325da manufacturing defects disable pes. reconfiguration can salvage dies, but remap logic costs area. design spare-row/column remapping for a systolic array with fault maps, implement rtl remap and a python yield analysis (defect density sweep). fault map remap logic yield analysis fault tolerance. units i, iv, v: biological robustness and self-organisation applied to hardware mbt325da-p3"}, {"id": "MBT325DB-P1", "title": "Fixed-Point Pan-Tompkins QRS Detector on MCU with PhysioNet Data", "course": "MBT325DB", "course_title": "Clinical Data Management", "sem": 2, "track": "B", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 58, "hay": "mbt325db-p1 fixed-point pan-tompkins qrs detector on mcu with physionet data clinical data management mbt325db wearables need low-power signal processing on constrained devices. implement pan-tompkins qrs detection in floating point, then fixed-point c, validate on public physionet ecg data (for example mit-bih), report sensitivity and positive predictivity, and measure cycles on an mcu model. filter chain detection logic fixed-point port evaluation signal processing on embedded. course-title topics (clinical signal data, privacy). the 2026 pdf does not print this course's own units; see the guide mbt325db-p1"}, {"id": "MBT325DB-P2", "title": "Hardware-Experiment Data Management Pipeline with Validation and Audit Trail", "course": "MBT325DB", "course_title": "Clinical Data Management", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 18, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 59, "hay": "mbt325db-p2 hardware-experiment data management pipeline with validation and audit trail clinical data management mbt325db ppa results across many runs become unreliable without schema, validation and provenance, much like clinical data quality control. design a schema and validation rules for eda/benchmark results, ingest all your project runs with provenance (tool versions, configs, git hash), detect anomalies, and generate audit reports. schema ingestion validation rules provenance reports data quality engineering. course-title topics (data cleaning, standards, integrity, auditability). the 2026 pdf does not print this course's own units mbt325db-p2"}, {"id": "MBT325DB-P3", "title": "Wearable ECG Arrhythmia Classifier Accelerator (INT8 1D-CNN)", "course": "MBT325DB", "course_title": "Clinical Data Management", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "9-12", "w0": 9, "total": 22, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 60, "hay": "mbt325db-p3 wearable ecg arrhythmia classifier accelerator (int8 1d-cnn) clinical data management mbt325db ultra-low-power inference for wearables needs specialised hardware; fairness and data issues matter. train a small 1d-cnn on public ecg data, quantise to int8, implement a streaming rtl accelerator, estimate energy per heartbeat classification, and discuss bias/ethics limits. 1d-cnn quantisation rtl energy estimate domain accelerator. course-title topics (healthcare analytics, ai and ml, ethics and bias). the 2026 pdf does not print this course's own units mbt325db-p3"}, {"id": "MCN325DR-P1", "title": "Cloud-Batch EDA Regression: Containerised Yosys/OpenROAD Jobs on Kubernetes with Cost per Run", "course": "MCN325DR", "course_title": "Cloud Computing", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 22, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 61, "hay": "mcn325dr-p1 cloud-batch eda regression: containerised yosys/openroad jobs on kubernetes with cost per run cloud computing mcn325dr design-space sweeps of an rtl-to-gds flow need hundreds of runs. a laptop takes days and a cloud cluster can take an hour, but only if jobs are containerised, scheduled sensibly and costed honestly. package yosys, openroad-flow-scripts and the sky130 pdk into a version-pinned container image. run a parameter sweep (utilisation, clock period, placement density) as kubernetes jobs on a local cluster (kind or minikube) and, if credits allow, on a managed cluster with autoscaling. store metrics in s3-compatible object storage and compute wall time, node-hours and cost per run against a local baseline. pinned container image sweep generator kubernetes job specs autoscaler object store for results dashboard + cost model containers, kubernetes batch jobs, reproducible eda, cloud cost analysis. units i-iii: virtualisation and containers, docker and kubernetes, elastic scaling, cloud-native batch workloads, ci/cd and monitoring; unit iv cost management "}, {"id": "MCN325DR-P2", "title": "Sensor-to-Cloud IoT Pipeline: RTOS Drivers, MQTT, Cloud Storage and Reliability Measurement", "course": "MCN325DR", "course_title": "Cloud Computing", "sem": 2, "track": "B", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 17, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 62, "hay": "mcn325dr-p2 sensor-to-cloud iot pipeline: rtos drivers, mqtt, cloud storage and reliability measurement cloud computing mcn325dr iot cloud platforms (unit v) are only as reliable as the path from the sensor to the bucket: drivers, buffering, network handling, the broker and the storage service. build sensor drivers on an rtos with local buffering and mqtt publish with qos, connect to a broker in the cloud (or a local mosquitto with the same configuration), store data in object storage with least-privilege credentials, inject packet loss and outages, and measure data loss and latency end to end. drivers buffer mqtt client cloud broker object storage writer fault injector embedded networking. unit v iot cloud platforms; unit ii storage and elastic resources; unit iv iam and encryption mme325dq-p1"}, {"id": "MCN325DR-P3", "title": "Cloud Inference Serving: Autoscaled Model Endpoint with a Latency SLO, Cost and Security Review", "course": "MCN325DR", "course_title": "Cloud Computing", "sem": 2, "track": "B", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 63, "hay": "mcn325dr-p3 cloud inference serving: autoscaled model endpoint with a latency slo, cost and security review cloud computing mcn325dr serving an ml model looks easy until the load varies. the engineering question is how to meet a latency target (for example p95 under 100 ms) at the lowest cost, and which security controls even a minimal deployment needs. serve a quantised image classifier (from mve315ab-p2 or a public model) through a containerised inference server behind a load balancer. compare a fixed vm, a kubernetes deployment with horizontal autoscaling, and a serverless container service, each driven by a load generator replaying a bursty trace. measure p50/p95/p99 latency, cold starts and cost, then add least-privilege iam, tls and secrets management and write a threat model. model + server container load generator (trace replay) fixed vm deployment kubernetes + hpa serverless container metrics + cost security controls cloud-native deployment, autoscaling, performance under load, cloud security basics. unit iii microservices, serverless, ci/cd and monitoring; unit ii load balancing and elastic scaling; unit iv iam, encryption and cost optimisation; unit v ai/ml in the cloud and at the edge "}, {"id": "MCV325DS-P1", "title": "Edge Crack Detector for Concrete Surfaces: Small INT8 CNN on a Microcontroller", "course": "MCV325DS", "course_title": "Structural Diagnostics, Repair and Retrofitting Techniques", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 19, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 64, "hay": "mcv325ds-p1 edge crack detector for concrete surfaces: small int8 cnn on a microcontroller structural diagnostics, repair and retrofitting techniques mcv325ds visual crack surveys are slow and subjective. a small on-device classifier could flag cracked surface patches during a site walkthrough, but only if it is accurate and fast on cheap hardware. train a small cnn on a public concrete-crack image dataset, quantise it to int8 and deploy it on an stm32f4/f7 or esp32-s3 class microcontroller with a camera (or run it on recorded images if no camera is available). measure accuracy, latency and energy per inference, and produce a crack map for a test wall by tiling images. dataset + split small cnn int8 quantisation mcu deployment tiling + crack map accuracy/latency/energy report tinyml deployment, dataset hygiene, edge ai measurement. unit i: visual inspection, crack identification and mapping of distress patterns; unit ii inspection and reporting "}, {"id": "MCV325DS-P2", "title": "Ultrasonic Pulse Velocity Timing Front End: Time-of-Flight on an MCU and an FPGA", "course": "MCV325DS", "course_title": "Structural Diagnostics, Repair and Retrofitting Techniques", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 19, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 65, "hay": "mcv325ds-p2 ultrasonic pulse velocity timing front end: time-of-flight on an mcu and an fpga structural diagnostics, repair and retrofitting techniques mcv325ds ultrasonic pulse velocity (upv) testing estimates concrete quality from the transit time of a pulse between two transducers. the electronics need sub-microsecond timing with good repeatability, which makes a clean embedded-timing problem. build a upv timing chain (pulser, 50-54 khz transducers or a signal-generator stand-in, amplifier and comparator) and measure time of flight with an mcu timer capture and with an fpga counter or a delay-line tdc. characterise resolution, jitter and repeatability on reference blocks of known path length, then compute pulse velocity with uncertainty. pulse generator receiver amplifier + comparator mcu timer capture fpga counter / tdc calibration blocks velocity + uncertainty precision timing, analog front ends, fpga timing circuits, measurement uncertainty. unit v: ultrasonic pulse velocity test, applications and limitations; unit ii tools, equipment and reporting "}, {"id": "MCV325DS-P3", "title": "Low-Power Structural Health Monitoring Node: Modal-Frequency Tracking from Accelerometer FFTs", "course": "MCV325DS", "course_title": "Structural Diagnostics, Repair and Retrofitting Techniques", "sem": 2, "track": "B", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 66, "hay": "mcv325ds-p3 low-power structural health monitoring node: modal-frequency tracking from accelerometer ffts structural diagnostics, repair and retrofitting techniques mcv325ds a change in a structure's natural frequencies can indicate stiffness loss from cracking or corrosion. a battery-powered node must detect small frequency shifts while running for months. build a node with a mems accelerometer and an mcu that wakes periodically, records vibration, computes an fft with peak interpolation to estimate the first modal frequencies, and transmits only summaries. validate on a lab beam with added mass or a saw cut that changes stiffness. measure frequency resolution, detection of a known shift and energy per measurement cycle, and project battery life. accelerometer driver duty-cycled acquisition windowed fft + peak interpolation change detector low-power radio summary energy model embedded dsp, low-power design, sensing and validation. units i, iv, v: distress identification, evaluation of structural deformation with sensors, diagnostics that support repair decisions (course outcomes co2 and co4) "}, {"id": "MCV325DT-P1", "title": "GPU Agent-Based Traffic Simulator (Car-Following Models in CUDA)", "course": "MCV325DT", "course_title": "Smart Mobility Systems", "sem": 2, "track": "B", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 18, "cuda": true, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 67, "hay": "mcv325dt-p1 gpu agent-based traffic simulator (car-following models in cuda) smart mobility systems mcv325dt traffic microsimulation scales with vehicle count and is data-parallel. implement an intelligent driver model car-following simulation: cpu baseline, cuda with structure-of-arrays, neighbour search via sorting; validate with fundamental diagram. vehicle state soa neighbour search update kernel validation parallel simulation. unit i traffic flow fundamentals (speed, flow, density); unit v digital twins for transportation mcv325de-p1"}, {"id": "MCV325DT-P2", "title": "Edge Vision Latency-Budget and Roofline Analysis for Vehicle Detection", "course": "MCV325DT", "course_title": "Smart Mobility Systems", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 68, "hay": "mcv325dt-p2 edge vision latency-budget and roofline analysis for vehicle detection smart mobility systems mcv325dt perception pipelines must meet latency; analytical modelling identifies bottlenecks. build a layerwise operations/bytes model of a tiny detection network (compute from layer shapes), apply a roofline model for candidate hardware, and compare predictions to measurements on available hardware. network parser cost model roofline measurement performance modelling. unit ii sensing (cameras, anpr), edge and cloud computing mcv325de-p2"}, {"id": "MCV325DT-P3", "title": "Adaptive Signal Controller FSM in RTL with SUMO Co-Simulation", "course": "MCV325DT", "course_title": "Smart Mobility Systems", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 69, "hay": "mcv325dt-p3 adaptive signal controller fsm in rtl with sumo co-simulation smart mobility systems mcv325dt real-time adaptive control is a hardware-friendly state machine problem; evaluation needs traffic simulation. implement actuated/webster-style controller in rtl, connect to sumo via traci and a python bridge, evaluate delay against fixed-time control. controller rtl bridge sumo scenario hw/sw co-simulation. unit iv adaptive traffic control (atms); unit i intersections and traffic signals mcv325de-p3"}, {"id": "MEC325DF-P1", "title": "Display Controller RTL: Timing Generator, Framebuffer DMA and Alpha Blender", "course": "MEC325DF", "course_title": "Human Machine Interface (HMI)", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 18, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 70, "hay": "mec325df-p1 display controller rtl: timing generator, framebuffer dma and alpha blender human machine interface (hmi) mec325df a display path is a real-time dma problem: underruns show as visual glitches. implement a vga/hdmi-style timing generator, framebuffer reader through axi burst dma, fifo, and alpha blending; simulate with a frame checker and measure underrun conditions. timing gen dma reader fifo blender frame checker dma and real-time streaming. units i, iii: hmi hardware architecture, 2d rendering and graphics mec325df-p1"}, {"id": "MEC325DF-P2", "title": "2D Rasteriser and Alpha Compositor on GPU: Frame-Time Budget Study", "course": "MEC325DF", "course_title": "Human Machine Interface (HMI)", "sem": 2, "track": "B", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 17, "cuda": true, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 71, "hay": "mec325df-p2 2d rasteriser and alpha compositor on gpu: frame-time budget study human machine interface (hmi) mec325df ui frame budgets (16.6 ms at 60 hz) require understanding of gpu compositing cost. write cuda (or opengl compute) kernels for tiled 2d compositing of layers; profile bandwidth; compare with cpu compositor. layer buffers compositor kernel profiler gpu graphics fundamentals. unit iii 2d/3d rendering and graphical assets; unit iv performance of interactive interfaces mec325df-p2"}, {"id": "MEC325DF-P3", "title": "Input-to-Photon Latency Measurement Framework on MCU + RTOS", "course": "MEC325DF", "course_title": "Human Machine Interface (HMI)", "sem": 2, "track": "B", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 72, "hay": "mec325df-p3 input-to-photon latency measurement framework on mcu + rtos human machine interface (hmi) mec325df perceived responsiveness depends on end-to-end latency, including interrupts and scheduling. build a framework with hardware timestamping (gpio/timer capture), rtos tasks for touch->render, and a statistical latency analysis with jitter attribution. timestamping rtos pipeline analysis profiling, rtos. units ii, iv: driver distraction and glance time, hmi testing methodologies mec325df-p3"}, {"id": "MEE325DG-P1", "title": "BMS Protection and Cell-Balancing FSM in RTL with Formal Properties", "course": "MEE325DG", "course_title": "Electric Vehicle Technology", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 73, "hay": "mee325dg-p1 bms protection and cell-balancing fsm in rtl with formal properties electric vehicle technology mee325dg bms protections are safety-critical state machines suited to formal checks. implement over/under-voltage, over-temperature, passive balancing, and contactor control fsm; formal properties for safety; co-sim with a cell model. monitors protection fsm balancer formal safety fsm design. unit iii bms objectives, charging and discharging control, cell balancing mee325dg-p1"}, {"id": "MEE325DG-P2", "title": "J1772 Pilot-Signal and Charging-Session State Machine", "course": "MEE325DG", "course_title": "Electric Vehicle Technology", "sem": 2, "track": "B", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 19, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 74, "hay": "mee325dg-p2 j1772 pilot-signal and charging-session state machine electric vehicle technology mee325dg charging control relies on a pilot pwm and state machine; correct timing is needed. implement an evse-side state machine measuring pilot duty/voltage, with rtos firmware and a protocol simulator testing all states and faults. pilot measure state machine simulator tests protocol firmware. unit v charging standards (sae j1772) and charger architectures mee325dg-p2"}, {"id": "MEE325DG-P3", "title": "Custom RISC-V Instructions for Clarke/Park Transforms", "course": "MEE325DG", "course_title": "Electric Vehicle Technology", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "8-10", "w0": 8, "total": 24, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 75, "hay": "mee325dg-p3 custom risc-v instructions for clarke/park transforms electric vehicle technology mee325dg custom instructions can accelerate control kernels; cost versus benefit needs measurement. extend your rv32i core (or ibex/picorv32) with custom instructions for clarke/park transforms and sin/cos lookup, modify the compiler assembly via intrinsics, and measure cycles and area. custom unit decode changes intrinsics benchmark isa extension. unit ii motor drive control blocks mee325dg-p3"}, {"id": "MET325DH-P1", "title": "GNSS C/A Acquisition: FFT-Based Parallel Code-Phase Search", "course": "MET325DH", "course_title": "Electronic Navigation Systems", "sem": 2, "track": "B", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 19, "cuda": true, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 76, "hay": "met325dh-p1 gnss c/a acquisition: fft-based parallel code-phase search electronic navigation systems met325dh acquisition is a correlation search that maps well to ffts and parallel hardware. implement acquisition in python on public gnss sample data, then optimise with a cuda fft or an optimised cpu fft; compare time and detection reliability. code generator fft correlator detector fft pipelines. unit iii gnss signals and receivers met325dh-p1"}, {"id": "MET325DH-P2", "title": "FMCW Radar Range-Doppler Pipeline: 2D FFT in CUDA and RTL", "course": "MET325DH", "course_title": "Electronic Navigation Systems", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 22, "cuda": true, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 77, "hay": "met325dh-p2 fmcw radar range-doppler pipeline: 2d fft in cuda and rtl electronic navigation systems met325dh radar processing is fft-heavy and bandwidth-bound; hardware vs gpu trade-offs apply. simulate fmcw chirps, implement range-doppler map with 2d fft in python, cuda, and a streaming rtl fft; compare throughput and resources. signal generator 2d fft cpu cuda rtl fft streaming fft. unit i radar detection and doppler met325dh-p2"}, {"id": "MET325DH-P3", "title": "LiDAR Point-Cloud Voxel Filter and Nearest-Neighbour Search on GPU", "course": "MET325DH", "course_title": "Electronic Navigation Systems", "sem": 2, "track": "B", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 20, "cuda": true, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 78, "hay": "met325dh-p3 lidar point-cloud voxel filter and nearest-neighbour search on gpu electronic navigation systems met325dh point-cloud processing is irregular and memory-bound; spatial structures decide performance. implement voxel-grid downsampling and nearest-neighbour via spatial hashing in cuda; compare with cpu kd-tree; profile memory behaviour. hash grid voxel kernel nn kernel profiler irregular gpu workloads. unit iv lidar data and processing met325dh-p3"}, {"id": "MIM325DK-P1", "title": "PERT/CPM Planner with Monte-Carlo Schedule Risk for a Tape-Out-Style Project", "course": "MIM325DK", "course_title": "Essentials of Project Management", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Beginner", "label": "Build first", "wk": "3-4", "w0": 3, "total": 17, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 79, "hay": "mim325dk-p1 pert/cpm planner with monte-carlo schedule risk for a tape-out-style project essentials of project management mim325dk chip projects slip because dependencies are underestimated. build a wbs and activity network for your major project, compute cpm, and run monte-carlo schedule simulation with triangular distributions. wbs cpm engine monte-carlo planning. unit iv gantt, cpm and pert; unit i wbs mim325dk-p1"}, {"id": "MIM325DK-P2", "title": "Financial Model for an AI Accelerator Chip (Parametric NRE and Break-Even)", "course": "MIM325DK", "course_title": "Essentials of Project Management", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "3-5", "w0": 3, "total": 16, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 80, "hay": "mim325dk-p2 financial model for an ai accelerator chip (parametric nre and break-even) essentials of project management mim325dk silicon investments depend on nre, unit cost and volume. parametric model: nre items, die cost from yield model, packaging, volume, price, npv, break-even. all numbers are user-entered assumptions with notes. cost inputs npv model sensitivity business reasoning. units ii, iii: capital budgeting, costing, financial modelling and sensitivity analysis mim325dk-p2"}, {"id": "MIM325DK-P3", "title": "CI/CD for Hardware: Regression, Coverage and PPA Tracking Pipeline", "course": "MIM325DK", "course_title": "Essentials of Project Management", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 18, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 81, "hay": "mim325dk-p3 ci/cd for hardware: regression, coverage and ppa tracking pipeline essentials of project management mim325dk hardware teams need continuous regression and ppa tracking to avoid late surprises. set up a ci pipeline (github actions) running verilator/cocotb tests, coverage, linting, synthesis and ppa trend tracking; measure team velocity metrics. test runner coverage synthesis job ppa dashboard engineering process. unit v agile methodology; unit iv computerised project management mim325dk-p3"}, {"id": "MIS325DM-P1", "title": "Interactive Design-Space Explorer for Accelerator Results with Usability Testing", "course": "MIS325DM", "course_title": "User Interface & User Experience", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Beginner", "label": "Build first", "wk": "3-5", "w0": 3, "total": 17, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 82, "hay": "mis325dm-p1 interactive design-space explorer for accelerator results with usability testing user interface & user experience mis325dm engineers must compare hundreds of design points; poor ui hides insight. build a static web app that explores a ppa design-space csv (filter, pareto, scatter), test with 5 users, iterate. data loader charts filters usability study ui engineering. units i-iii: screen types and patterns, navigation, visual design mis325dm-p1"}, {"id": "MIS325DM-P2", "title": "Timing-Report and Waveform Viewer for Web", "course": "MIS325DM", "course_title": "User Interface & User Experience", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 18, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 83, "hay": "mis325dm-p2 timing-report and waveform viewer for web user interface & user experience mis325dm debugging needs fast visual exploration of vcd waveforms and sta paths. create a browser viewer for vcd subsets and opensta path reports, with search, zoom, and keyboard accessibility. vcd parser renderer path view tooling. units ii, iii: navigation, layout and visual patterns mis325dm-p2"}, {"id": "MIS325DM-P3", "title": "Register-Map Generator: YAML to RTL, C Headers, Docs and Web Explorer", "course": "MIS325DM", "course_title": "User Interface & User Experience", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 84, "hay": "mis325dm-p3 register-map generator: yaml to rtl, c headers, docs and web explorer user interface & user experience mis325dm register maps drift between rtl, drivers and docs. a single source of truth prevents bugs. define yaml csr spec; generate axi-lite register rtl, c header, markdown docs, and an interactive web explorer; verify with generated tests. prior tools exist (for example peakrdl); this is a learning implementation. spec schema rtl gen c gen docs/web tests hw/sw interface. unit iv ui systems and atomic design; unit i information architecture mis325dm-p3"}, {"id": "MMA325DN-P1", "title": "Quantisation Error Analysis: SQNR Theory vs Measurement and Optimal Clipping", "course": "MMA325DN", "course_title": "Mathematical Methods for Data Science", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "3-5", "w0": 3, "total": 23, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 85, "hay": "mma325dn-p1 quantisation error analysis: sqnr theory vs measurement and optimal clipping mathematical methods for data science mma325dn quantisation scale choice is an optimisation problem; theory predicts sqnr. derive sqnr for uniform quantisers; measure on real weight/activation distributions; find clipping thresholds by mse minimisation and kl-style search; compare. distribution fit quantiser optimiser evaluation numerical analysis. unit i estimation; units ii-iii optimisation mma325dn-p1"}, {"id": "MMA325DN-P2", "title": "Surrogate-Model Optimisation of Accelerator PPA from Synthesis Data", "course": "MMA325DN", "course_title": "Mathematical Methods for Data Science", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 25, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 86, "hay": "mma325dn-p2 surrogate-model optimisation of accelerator ppa from synthesis data mathematical methods for data science mma325dn running synthesis for every design point is slow. surrogates can guide the search. generate ppa data by synthesising parameterised arrays, train regression surrogates, and use bayesian optimisation to find pareto designs; compare with random search. data generation surrogates bo comparison ml for design. units ii, iii, v: optimisation and regression mma325dn-p2"}, {"id": "MMA325DN-P3", "title": "Reduced-Precision Number Formats: bfloat16, FP8 and Block Floating Point Emulation", "course": "MMA325DN", "course_title": "Mathematical Methods for Data Science", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "8-10", "w0": 8, "total": 25, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 87, "hay": "mma325dn-p3 reduced-precision number formats: bfloat16, fp8 and block floating point emulation mathematical methods for data science mma325dn number format choice determines accelerator area and accuracy. emulation allows study without silicon. emulate int8, bfloat16, fp8 (e4m3/e5m2 as in micikevicius et al., 2022), and block floating point for matmul, softmax and layernorm of a small transformer; relate accuracy to estimated mac area. format emulators layer tests area model numerics. units iii, iv: optimisation, neural networks and loss functions mma325dn-p3"}, {"id": "MME325DU-P1", "title": "QFD and Morphological-Chart Design of a Wearable Biopotential Front End", "course": "MME325DU", "course_title": "Creative Engineering", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Beginner", "label": "Build first", "wk": "3-4", "w0": 3, "total": 15, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 88, "hay": "mme325du-p1 qfd and morphological-chart design of a wearable biopotential front end creative engineering mme325du engineering students tend to jump to a favourite circuit before understanding the requirements. a structured design method shows how user needs turn into specifications such as input-referred noise, power and size. apply the course's design process to a wearable ecg/emg front end: interview 3-5 potential users (clinicians, athletes, fellow students), build a requirements list and a qfd house of quality, decompose functions, generate alternatives with a morphological chart (electrodes, amplifier topology, adc, filtering, power source), and evaluate concepts with weighted scoring. back the top two concepts with quick calculations or simulations. user interviews requirements list qfd matrix function structure morphological chart weighted evaluation requirements engineering, qfd, structured concept generation and selection. unit ii: task clarification, requirements identification, qfd; unit iii functional decomposition and morphological charts; unit v concept evaluation and selection "}, {"id": "MME325DU-P2", "title": "TRIZ for an Accelerator Memory-Bandwidth Contradiction: Three Concepts Evaluated in Simulation", "course": "MME325DU", "course_title": "Creative Engineering", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 89, "hay": "mme325du-p2 triz for an accelerator memory-bandwidth contradiction: three concepts evaluated in simulation creative engineering mme325du accelerator designers face classic contradictions: larger on-chip buffers cut dram traffic but cost area and energy; more pes raise peak throughput but starve for bandwidth. triz offers a systematic way to look for solutions rather than only trading one off against the other. state the contradiction for a gemm accelerator (raising throughput worsens memory-bandwidth demand). use the contradiction matrix and inventive principles to generate concepts (segmentation into tiles, prior action through prefetch, local quality through mixed precision, nesting through hierarchical buffers). model three concepts with an analytical traffic and energy model (or the tile scheduler of mve315ab-p1), compare them with ordinal and cardinal methods, and select one. problem model contradiction matrix inventive principles concept set analytical simulation ordinal + cardinal evaluation systematic innovation, analytical modelling, design evaluation. unit iv: contradiction analysis, triz techniques, analytical and virtual design simulation; unit v ordinal and cardinal concept evaluation and selection "}, {"id": "MME325DU-P3", "title": "Concept Selection with Virtual Prototyping: Thermal Solutions for an Edge AI Module", "course": "MME325DU", "course_title": "Creative Engineering", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 19, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 90, "hay": "mme325du-p3 concept selection with virtual prototyping: thermal solutions for an edge ai module creative engineering mme325du an edge ai module must dissipate several watts in a small sealed box. choosing between a heat sink, a spreader to the enclosure, a fan or throttling is a real concept-selection problem that rewards simulation before building. define requirements (junction temperature limit, ambient, size, noise, cost). generate concepts, screen them with thermal-resistance models, simulate the survivors with an open finite-element tool, and test the top concept physically with a heater resistor or a real board and a thermocouple or thermal camera. compare simulated and measured temperatures and use both in the final evaluation. requirements concept generation thermal-resistance models fem simulation physical prototype test evaluation and selection thermal design basics, fem, virtual prototyping, design evaluation. unit iv: analytical, virtual and physical simulation of design concepts; unit v ordinal and cardinal evaluation and concept selection; unit ii requirements "}, {"id": "MME325DV-P1", "title": "TinyML Vibration Anomaly Detector on MCU", "course": "MME325DV", "course_title": "Industry 5.0 - Human-centric, Sustainable and Resilient Manufacturing", "sem": 2, "track": "B", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 91, "hay": "mme325dv-p1 tinyml vibration anomaly detector on mcu industry 5.0 - human-centric, sustainable and resilient manufacturing mme325dv predictive maintenance in a resilient factory runs at the edge with tight memory and must flag anomalies before failures stop production. train an autoencoder/classifier on public vibration datasets, quantise to int8, deploy with c runtime, measure memory and latency. feature extraction model runtime evaluation tinyml. unit iii machine learning for anomaly detection and edge iiot; unit v predictive analytics mme325do-p1"}, {"id": "MME325DV-P2", "title": "Cobot Speed-and-Separation Monitoring Interlock: Dual-Channel Safety FSM with Formal Proofs", "course": "MME325DV", "course_title": "Industry 5.0 - Human-centric, Sustainable and Resilient Manufacturing", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 92, "hay": "mme325dv-p2 cobot speed-and-separation monitoring interlock: dual-channel safety fsm with formal proofs industry 5.0 - human-centric, sustainable and resilient manufacturing mme325dv in human-robot collaboration a cobot must slow down or stop when a person comes too close (speed and separation monitoring in iso/ts 15066). the interlock logic must be provably correct, and formal methods give strong evidence. design a dual-channel interlock fsm that takes two independent distance sensors, computes the protective separation distance for the current robot speed, and commands normal, reduced-speed or protective stop, with voting, a watchdog and safe defaults when the sensors disagree. prove safety properties with symbiyosys and test with injected sensor faults. dual-channel distance inputs separation-distance check voting watchdog mode fsm formal properties safety design, formal verification, human-robot collaboration requirements. unit ii cobot safety requirements (iso/ts 15066) and human-robot collaboration system design mcv325dd-p1"}, {"id": "MME325DV-P3", "title": "Edge Predictive-Maintenance SoC Reference Architecture and HW/SW Partition Study", "course": "MME325DV", "course_title": "Industry 5.0 - Human-centric, Sustainable and Resilient Manufacturing", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 93, "hay": "mme325dv-p3 edge predictive-maintenance soc reference architecture and hw/sw partition study industry 5.0 - human-centric, sustainable and resilient manufacturing mme325dv a resilient, human-centric factory pushes analytics to the edge. choosing what runs on the cpu and what goes to an accelerator in an edge predictive-maintenance node drives cost, power and response time. define workload (fft, features, small nn), profile on cpu model, evaluate partition options with analytical latency/energy models, and produce an architecture document. workload profiler partition model architecture doc hw/sw partitioning. unit iii cyber-physical production systems, digital twins, edge computing and iiot architectures mme325do-p2"}, {"id": "MIM426RT-P1", "title": "Systematic Literature Map: INT8/INT4 Systolic and Dataflow Accelerators", "course": "MIM426RT", "course_title": "Research Methodology (MOOC)", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 94, "hay": "mim426rt-p1 systematic literature map: int8/int4 systolic and dataflow accelerators research methodology (mooc) mim426rt without a structured survey, projects repeat known work. build a protocol-driven literature map (search strings, inclusion rules, tagged bibtex database) of at least 30 papers, classify by dataflow, precision, memory, and evaluation methodology, and identify genuine gaps. verify every citation exists before including it. protocol search screening classification gap analysis research skills. weeks 1-2: literature survey; week 4: technical writing mim426rt-p1"}, {"id": "MIM426RT-P2", "title": "Design of Experiments for EDA Flow Parameters (Factorial/Taguchi with ANOVA)", "course": "MIM426RT", "course_title": "Research Methodology (MOOC)", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 95, "hay": "mim426rt-p2 design of experiments for eda flow parameters (factorial/taguchi with anova) research methodology (mooc) mim426rt tuning eda flows by trial and error wastes time; doe gives structured evidence. choose 5 flow parameters, run a fractional factorial or taguchi design on openroad across designs and seeds, analyse main effects and interactions with anova. design matrix runner anova confirmation runs experimental method. week 3: data analysis; week 6: design of experiments mim426rt-p2"}, {"id": "MIM426RT-P3", "title": "Artifact-Style Replication of a Published Accelerator Energy Model + IP/Licensing Review", "course": "MIM426RT", "course_title": "Research Methodology (MOOC)", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 96, "hay": "mim426rt-p3 artifact-style replication of a published accelerator energy model + ip/licensing review research methodology (mooc) mim426rt reproducibility and licensing are part of professional research. replicate an energy/latency analysis using timeloop-style analytical modelling (parashar et al., ispass 2019) on a layer set; write artifact-evaluation style documentation; review licences (apache-2.0, cern-ohl, gpl) of tools you reuse. replication artifact docs licence review reproducibility. weeks 5-7: ethics, creativity, intellectual property mim426rt-p3"}, {"id": "HSS227TA-P1", "title": "Carbon Accounting for Your Compute: Energy and CO2e of EDA Runs and Model Training", "course": "HSS227TA", "course_title": "Environment and Sustainability", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Beginner", "label": "Build first", "wk": "2-3", "w0": 2, "total": 17, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 97, "hay": "hss227ta-p1 carbon accounting for your compute: energy and co2e of eda runs and model training environment and sustainability hss227ta design sweeps and model training consume real energy, but students rarely know how much. measuring it turns a sustainability lecture into an engineering metric you can optimise. measure the energy of representative workloads (an openroad flow, a pytorch training run, a cuda benchmark) with rapl counters, nvidia-smi or a smart plug, convert to co2e with the current indian grid emission factor published by the central electricity authority, and test two reduction strategies (fewer sweep points through design of experiments; lower precision or early stopping). workload set energy measurement emission factor reduction experiments guideline energy measurement, carbon accounting, experiment design. unit ii: energy efficiency, energy auditing and management, carbon concepts and reduction strategies; unit iii sustainability reporting "}, {"id": "HSS227TA-P2", "title": "Screening Life-Cycle Assessment of an Edge AI Device: Embodied vs Operational Carbon", "course": "HSS227TA", "course_title": "Environment and Sustainability", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "3-5", "w0": 3, "total": 18, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 98, "hay": "hss227ta-p2 screening life-cycle assessment of an edge ai device: embodied vs operational carbon environment and sustainability hss227ta for small electronic devices, manufacturing the chips can emit more carbon than years of use. knowing which phase dominates changes design decisions such as die size, process node and product lifetime. run a screening lca (iso 14040-style goal and scope, inventory and impact) of an edge ai device such as a microcontroller board with a camera or a small gpu module. estimate embodied carbon from published per-area semiconductor data, pcb and packaging, and operational carbon from measured power under usage scenarios. run sensitivity analysis on lifetime, grid factor and die area and recommend design changes. goal and scope bill of materials embodied carbon model use-phase measurement sensitivity analysis recommendations lca method, data sourcing, uncertainty analysis. unit iii: life-cycle assessment, sustainable materials and manufacturing, resource recovery; unit i sustainable development principles "}, {"id": "HSS227TA-P3", "title": "Energy Audit of a Teaching Lab: Logged Loads, Idle Waste and a Verified Saving", "course": "HSS227TA", "course_title": "Environment and Sustainability", "sem": 2, "track": "B", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "3-5", "w0": 3, "total": 16, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 99, "hay": "hss227ta-p3 energy audit of a teaching lab: logged loads, idle waste and a verified saving environment and sustainability hss227ta labs leave workstations, oscilloscopes and fpga boards powered all day. an audit with real data shows where the energy goes and whether a simple control measure saves anything. with permission from the lab in-charge, log the power of representative equipment for two weeks with smart plugs or a clamp meter, build load profiles, estimate idle waste, implement one measure (scheduled shutdown, auto-suspend policy or a smart power strip), and measure the saving over another two weeks with a before/after comparison normalised for usage. equipment inventory power logging load profiles idle-waste estimate intervention before/after analysis energy auditing, measurement planning, before/after analysis. unit ii: energy efficiency and conservation strategies, energy auditing and energy management; unit iii sustainability policies "}, {"id": "MVE231TA-P1", "title": "UVM Agent, Sequences and Scoreboard for an AXI-Stream FIFO", "course": "MVE231TA", "course_title": "Universal Verification Methodology (UVM)", "sem": 3, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 22, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 100, "hay": "mve231ta-p1 uvm agent, sequences and scoreboard for an axi-stream fifo universal verification methodology (uvm) mve231ta class-based systemverilog testbenches work, but they are hard to reuse across projects and teams. uvm imposes a standard structure; the question is what it costs and what it gives back on a small, well-understood block. port the constrained-random testbench of mve222ia-p1 to uvm: an axi-stream agent (sequencer, driver, monitor) on each side, a reference model and scoreboard connected through tlm analysis ports, configuration through uvm_config_db, and factory overrides to swap sequences and inject errors. run it on a uvm-capable simulator, or with pyuvm on cocotb if none is available, and compare effort and bugs found with the original testbench. axi-stream agent (input) axi-stream agent (output) sequence library reference model scoreboard (tlm) environment + tests factory overrides uvm architecture, tlm, sequences, factory and configuration database. units i-ii: uvm base class library, testbench architecture, phases, tlm and reporting; sequences, sequencer, driver, monitor, configuration database and factory "}, {"id": "MVE231TA-P2", "title": "RAL-Driven Verification of Accelerator CSRs with Coverage Closure", "course": "MVE231TA", "course_title": "Universal Verification Methodology (UVM)", "sem": 3, "track": "C", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 24, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 101, "hay": "mve231ta-p2 ral-driven verification of accelerator csrs with coverage closure universal verification methodology (uvm) mve231ta accelerator control registers are where software meets hardware. bugs in access policies, reset values or side effects break drivers silently. a uvm register model generated from the register specification checks all of this systematically. generate a uvm ral model from the yaml register map of mis325dm-p3 (or from systemrdl with peakrdl), connect it through an axi-lite adapter and predictor to the accelerator csr block, and run the built-in register sequences (reset, bit-bash, access) plus custom sequences for side effects (start bits, write-1-to-clear status, interrupt masks). write covergroups for fields and access combinations and close coverage with targeted sequences, documenting every exclusion. register spec (yaml or systemrdl) ral generator axi-lite agent + adapter predictor + mirror built-in register sequences side-effect sequences coverage model uvm ral, coverage closure, register specifications, hw/sw interface verification. unit iii: coverage-driven verification, functional coverage, assertions, scoreboards, uvm register abstraction layer (mirroring, block configuration, adapters), coverage closure; unit iv soc and protocol verification "}, {"id": "MVE231TA-P3", "title": "ML-Guided Coverage Closure vs Constrained Random in UVM: A Controlled Study", "course": "MVE231TA", "course_title": "Universal Verification Methodology (UVM)", "sem": 3, "track": "C", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "8-10", "w0": 8, "total": 27, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 102, "hay": "mve231ta-p3 ml-guided coverage closure vs constrained random in uvm: a controlled study universal verification methodology (uvm) mve231ta the last few percent of functional coverage consume a large part of a verification schedule. published work suggests ml can steer stimulus towards uncovered bins, but results depend heavily on the design and the baseline. a controlled study on open designs tests whether it helps. pick two open designs with non-trivial coverage models (for example an axi crossbar and the decoder and hazard logic of an rv32 core). build a uvm or pyuvm testbench that exposes sequence knobs (constraint weights, lengths, ordering). compare plain constrained random, a coverage-feedback heuristic, bayesian optimisation over the knobs, and an llm that proposes sequences from the uncovered-bin report, each under the same simulation budget and repeated seeds. designs + coverage models knob-exposed sequences random baseline feedback heuristic bayesian optimiser llm sequence proposer statistics coverage closure, ml for verification, experiment design, llm evaluation. unit v: machine learning for verification engineers, ai-driven test generation, ai-assisted coverage closure, llms for uvm verification; unit iii coverage closure "}, {"id": "MVE332CA-P1", "title": "Diffusion and Deal-Grove Oxidation Simulators", "course": "MVE332CA", "course_title": "Semiconductor Manufacturing", "sem": 3, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "3-5", "w0": 3, "total": 17, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 103, "hay": "mve332ca-p1 diffusion and deal-grove oxidation simulators semiconductor manufacturing mve332ca process simulators hide assumptions; building simple ones clarifies them. implement 1d diffusion (finite difference) and deal-grove oxidation in python; validate against analytical solutions; explore dopant profiles. fd solver deal-grove validation process modelling. unit ii diffusion (fick) and deal-grove oxidation mve324c4-p1"}, {"id": "MVE332CA-P2", "title": "Die Cost and Yield Model: Monolithic vs Chiplet Accelerator", "course": "MVE332CA", "course_title": "Semiconductor Manufacturing", "sem": 3, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 104, "hay": "mve332ca-p2 die cost and yield model: monolithic vs chiplet accelerator semiconductor manufacturing mve332ca die size drives cost through yield and wafer packing. architects need such models. build a parametric die-per-wafer and yield (poisson/negative binomial) cost model, compare monolithic accelerator with chiplet splits including packaging overhead as explicit assumptions. wafer packing yield models cost model sensitivity cost/yield reasoning. unit v chiplets and 3d integration; unit i crystal defects and yield mve324c4-p2"}, {"id": "MVE332CA-P3", "title": "Virtual-Wafer Variation to Speed Binning of Accelerator Dies", "course": "MVE332CA", "course_title": "Semiconductor Manufacturing", "sem": 3, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "7-9", "w0": 7, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 105, "hay": "mve332ca-p3 virtual-wafer variation to speed binning of accelerator dies semiconductor manufacturing mve332ca parametric yield depends on spatial and random variation. speed binning decides revenue. generate virtual wafers with systematic and random parameter variation, map to path delay and leakage using your spice/sta models, simulate speed bins, and analyse with control charts. wafer generator delay/leakage mapping binning spc charts statistical process thinking. unit v virtual metrology and ml in fabs; unit i defects and yield mve324c4-p3"}, {"id": "MVE332CB-P1", "title": "Biopotential Acquisition Subsystem for a Healthcare SoC: ADC Interface, FIR Decimation and Lossless Compression", "course": "MVE332CB", "course_title": "Healthcare SoCs", "sem": 3, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 22, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 106, "hay": "mve332cb-p1 biopotential acquisition subsystem for a healthcare soc: adc interface, fir decimation and lossless compression healthcare socs mve332cb wearable ecg and eeg socs spend much of their energy moving and transmitting data. filtering and compressing next to the sensor cuts radio energy, but the hardware must keep up in real time and stay bit-exact. build an acquisition subsystem in rtl: an spi master for a biopotential adc (ads1292 or ads1299 class, or a recorded-data emulator), a polyphase fir decimator, and a lossless delta plus adaptive rice encoder, with axi-lite control and axi-stream output to a soft cpu or dma. verify against a python model on physionet recordings and measure compression ratio, latency and fpga resources. spi adc master / data emulator polyphase fir decimator delta + rice encoder axi-lite csrs axi-stream to cpu/dma python golden model dsp in rtl, soc interfaces, data compression, verification against golden models. unit v: real-time signal acquisition and compression; unit ii soc components and interfaces; unit iv peripheral interfaces and high-density fpgas "}, {"id": "MVE332CB-P2", "title": "Bus vs Network-on-Chip for a Wearable Health SoC: Transaction-Level Model with Power Estimates", "course": "MVE332CB", "course_title": "Healthcare SoCs", "sem": 3, "track": "C", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 23, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 107, "hay": "mve332cb-p2 bus vs network-on-chip for a wearable health soc: transaction-level model with power estimates healthcare socs mve332cb a wearable health soc combines a cpu, a dsp or ai accelerator, sensors, memory and a radio. whether a shared bus is enough or a small noc is worth its area depends on the traffic, and that should be decided with a model before any rtl. build a transaction-level model in systemc (or python simpy) of a health soc with a risc-v cpu, an ecg/eeg accelerator, dma, sram banks, sensor interfaces and a ble radio. model an ahb-style shared bus, a crossbar and a 2x2 mesh noc, drive them with traffic from realistic workloads (acquisition, inference bursts, radio packets), and estimate area and energy per transfer from published or synthesised numbers. report latency, utilisation and energy for each option. workload traffic generators systemc tlm components shared-bus model crossbar model 2x2 mesh noc model power/area estimates comparison soc architecture, transaction-level modelling, interconnect trade-offs. unit ii: bus-based, noc and hybrid soc architectures, components, and power, area and performance considerations; unit i soc design goals and system-level design issues "}, {"id": "MVE332CB-P3", "title": "Tamper-Evident Audit Logger for a Medical-Device SoC: SHA-256 Hash Chain in RTL", "course": "MVE332CB", "course_title": "Healthcare SoCs", "sem": 3, "track": "C", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "7-9", "w0": 7, "total": 22, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 108, "hay": "mve332cb-p3 tamper-evident audit logger for a medical-device soc: sha-256 hash chain in rtl healthcare socs mve332cb medical devices must keep trustworthy records of configuration changes, alarms and firmware updates for safety investigations and audits. a hardware hash-chained logger makes undetected tampering much harder, which is part of the threat model unit v asks for. write a short threat model (stride) for a wearable health soc, then design an rtl event logger with a sha-256 hash-chained log (using a vetted open core or your own verified implementation), an axi-lite interface, and host software that verifies the chain and detects tampering. threat model event capture sha-256 core log memory axi-lite chain verifier secure soc components. unit v threat modelling for medical devices; unit iv soc security mcn325dc-p3"}, {"id": "MVE332CC-P1", "title": "FOC Motor-Control Pipeline in RTL (Clarke, Park, PI, SVPWM)", "course": "MVE332CC", "course_title": "Autonomous Systems and Electric Vehicles", "sem": 3, "track": "C", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build first", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 109, "hay": "mve332cc-p1 foc motor-control pipeline in rtl (clarke, park, pi, svpwm) autonomous systems and electric vehicles mve332cc traction drives need deterministic microsecond control loops. a hardware foc pipeline gives fixed latency that software on a busy mcu cannot guarantee, and the same blocks appear in every ev inverter controller. implement clarke, park, pi controllers and svpwm in fixed-point verilog with a python or verilator motor-plant model in the loop. measure loop latency, quantisation effects and stability. adc interface model clarke/park pi svpwm plant model hil harness fixed-point control hardware, co-simulation. unit i traction motors (pmsm), traction inverters and drivetrain control mpe214a3-p2"}, {"id": "MVE332CC-P2", "title": "Battery SoC Estimation: EKF in Fixed-Point C on an MCU", "course": "MVE332CC", "course_title": "Autonomous Systems and Electric Vehicles", "sem": 3, "track": "B", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 110, "hay": "mve332cc-p2 battery soc estimation: ekf in fixed-point c on an mcu autonomous systems and electric vehicles mve332cc coulomb counting drifts. an extended kalman filter fixes it but must run in real time on a small mcu in fixed point. build an equivalent-circuit battery model, validate on a public cell dataset, implement coulomb counting and an ekf in float then fixed point, and run on an mcu or emulator with timing measured. cell model ekf fixed-point conversion mcu harness evaluation state estimation, fixed-point embedded implementation. unit ii soc estimation with ekf, equivalent-circuit models and coulomb counting mpe214a3-p1"}, {"id": "MVE332CC-P3", "title": "Camera-LiDAR Late Fusion with EKF Tracking and Pure Pursuit/Stanley Control: A Closed-Loop Latency Budget", "course": "MVE332CC", "course_title": "Autonomous Systems and Electric Vehicles", "sem": 3, "track": "B", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "8-10", "w0": 8, "total": 23, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 111, "hay": "mve332cc-p3 camera-lidar late fusion with ekf tracking and pure pursuit/stanley control: a closed-loop latency budget autonomous systems and electric vehicles mve332cc autonomy stacks are judged by closed-loop behaviour, not by detector accuracy alone. latency and fusion errors in perception propagate into tracking error and unsafe control. this project measures that chain end to end. in the carla simulator (or a lighter 2-d simulator if carla is too heavy), build a pipeline: a camera detector and lidar clustering, late fusion with an ekf multi-object tracker, a reference path, and pure pursuit and stanley controllers. inject sensor latency and noise and measure lateral tracking error and minimum time-to-collision. profile each stage on an embedded target (a jetson-class board if available, otherwise a cpu with stated specifications) to build a latency budget. simulator scenarios camera detector lidar clustering ekf tracker (late fusion) path + controllers latency/noise injection latency profiler sensor fusion, multi-object tracking, vehicle control, real-time budgeting. unit iii: perception hardware, 3d detection, point-cloud segmentation, early vs late fusion with ekf/ukf; unit iv localisation and path planning; unit v pure pursuit, stanley and mpc lateral control "}, {"id": "MVE332CD-P1", "title": "Package Power-Delivery Network: Target Impedance and Decoupling-Capacitor Optimisation for an Accelerator Die", "course": "MVE332CD", "course_title": "Semiconductor IC Packaging", "sem": 3, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 22, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 112, "hay": "mve332cd-p1 package power-delivery network: target impedance and decoupling-capacitor optimisation for an accelerator die semiconductor ic packaging mve332cd a burst of mac activity draws large current steps, and package inductance turns them into supply droop that costs frequency. the target-impedance method tells you how much decoupling you need and where. model the power-delivery path from the vrm through board, package (bumps, planes, vias) and on-die capacitance as an rlc ladder with published or estimated values. compute the target impedance from supply voltage, allowed ripple and current step, sweep the impedance profile in ngspice, and optimise package and board decoupling capacitors (values and counts) to stay under the target. check in the time domain with a current step derived from mac-array activity. current profile from rtl activity rlc pdn ladder target impedance ac impedance sweep capacitor optimiser transient droop check power integrity, pdn modelling, optimisation. unit ii: package power distribution, power-supply noise, inductive effects and decoupling-capacitor design; unit iv embedded passives "}, {"id": "MVE332CD-P2", "title": "Compact Thermal Model of a Flip-Chip Package: RC Network vs FEM, with Hot-Spot Throttling", "course": "MVE332CD", "course_title": "Semiconductor IC Packaging", "sem": 3, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 24, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 113, "hay": "mve332cd-p2 compact thermal model of a flip-chip package: rc network vs fem, with hot-spot throttling semiconductor ic packaging mve332cd accelerator hot spots can exceed safe junction temperatures long before the average power suggests a problem. designers need a fast thermal model to explore spreaders, tims and throttling policies; full fem is too slow for that loop. build a compact thermal rc network for a flip-chip package (die, tim, lid or spreader, heat sink) with a gridded die that captures hot spots from a floorplan power map. validate steady-state and transient responses against an fem model (elmer fem or fenics). then use the compact model to evaluate tim and spreader options and a temperature-triggered throttling policy on a workload power trace. floorplan power map gridded die rc network package layer stack fem reference model validation throttling simulation thermal modelling, model validation, thermal-aware design. unit ii: heat transfer in packaged systems, thermal interface materials, heat spreaders, thermal vias, chip- and module-level thermal management; unit iv flip-chip assembly "}, {"id": "MVE332CD-P3", "title": "Chiplet Die-to-Die Link Study: Silicon Interposer vs Organic Substrate for a Split Accelerator", "course": "MVE332CD", "course_title": "Semiconductor IC Packaging", "sem": 3, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "8-10", "w0": 8, "total": 25, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 114, "hay": "mve332cd-p3 chiplet die-to-die link study: silicon interposer vs organic substrate for a split accelerator semiconductor ic packaging mve332cd splitting an accelerator into chiplets improves yield, but the die-to-die links add bandwidth limits, energy per bit and latency that can erase the benefit. the question is which packaging option supports a given split. model die-to-die channels for a silicon interposer (fine-pitch micro-bumps, short rdl wires) and an organic substrate (coarser bumps, longer traces) as lossy transmission lines with neighbour crosstalk. simulate eye diagrams of a simple parallel link at several data rates, estimate energy per bit and bandwidth density using published die-to-die interface parameters (for example from the ucie specification), and evaluate the traffic of a two-chiplet gemm accelerator to decide which option meets its bandwidth at acceptable energy. channel geometry transmission-line models eye-diagram simulation energy-per-bit model bandwidth density accelerator traffic model decision analysis signal integrity, advanced packaging, chiplet system trade-offs. unit iv: fan-out and 3d packaging, through-silicon vias, redistribution layers, 3d ic stacking; unit ii signal distribution, transmission-line behaviour and crosstalk; unit i 3d systems integration "}, {"id": "MVE333DA-P1", "title": "Sensor Interface and Calibration Firmware (Thermistor/MEMS) with Accuracy Study", "course": "MVE333DA", "course_title": "Sensor Technologies: Physics, Fabrication, and Circuits", "sem": 3, "track": "B", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "3-5", "w0": 3, "total": 16, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 115, "hay": "mve333da-p1 sensor interface and calibration firmware (thermistor/mems) with accuracy study sensor technologies: physics, fabrication, and circuits mve333da raw adc counts need calibration and filtering. implement adc reading, linearisation (steinhart-hart or polynomial), noise filtering and temperature compensation on an mcu; evaluate accuracy. adc driver calibration filter sensor firmware. weeks 1-2, 8: sensor basics and circuits mve232e4-p1"}, {"id": "MVE333DA-P2", "title": "On-Sensor TinyML: Accelerometer Activity Classifier with Energy per Inference", "course": "MVE333DA", "course_title": "Sensor Technologies: Physics, Fabrication, and Circuits", "sem": 3, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 116, "hay": "mve333da-p2 on-sensor tinyml: accelerometer activity classifier with energy per inference sensor technologies: physics, fabrication, and circuits mve333da always-on sensing needs sub-mw inference. train a small classifier on public imu datasets, quantise, implement in c on mcu or risc-v softcore model, estimate energy per inference. features model c inference energy model edge ml. weeks 3-5, 8: sensor physics and systems mve232e4-p2"}, {"id": "MVE333DA-P3", "title": "Capacitive Readout Chain: Switched-Capacitor Front End + CIC Filter RTL", "course": "MVE333DA", "course_title": "Sensor Technologies: Physics, Fabrication, and Circuits", "sem": 3, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "7-9", "w0": 7, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 117, "hay": "mve333da-p3 capacitive readout chain: switched-capacitor front end + cic filter rtl sensor technologies: physics, fabrication, and circuits mve333da readout noise vs power defines sensor quality. model a capacitive sensor front-end in ngspice, digitise with a behavioural sigma-delta, implement decimation (cic) in rtl, and analyse noise vs power. front-end modulator model cic rtl noise analysis mixed-signal. weeks 6-8: fabrication and characterisation, sensor circuits mve232e4-p3"}, {"id": "MVE333DB-P1", "title": "Cache and MESI Coherence Simulator with Litmus Tests", "course": "MVE333DB", "course_title": "Multicore Computer Architecture", "sem": 3, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 22, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 118, "hay": "mve333db-p1 cache and mesi coherence simulator with litmus tests multicore computer architecture mve333db coherence bugs are subtle; a simulator with litmus tests builds protocol understanding. implement a multicore cache hierarchy with mesi in python/c++, run synthetic and trace workloads, verify invariants and litmus tests. cache model mesi fsm bus/directory invariant checker coherence. weeks 6-8: caches, optimisations, coherence mve341f4-p1"}, {"id": "MVE333DB-P2", "title": "Wormhole Mesh NoC Router RTL with Virtual Channels and Traffic Generators", "course": "MVE333DB", "course_title": "Multicore Computer Architecture", "sem": 3, "track": "C", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "7-10", "w0": 7, "total": 28, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 119, "hay": "mve333db-p2 wormhole mesh noc router rtl with virtual channels and traffic generators multicore computer architecture mve333db noc routers determine on-chip bandwidth and latency in multi-core and accelerator socs. implement a 5-port wormhole router (xy routing, credit flow control, virtual channels), build a 4x4 mesh, drive with traffic patterns, verify no deadlock, and measure latency-throughput curves vs a booksim 2 model of the same mesh. input buffers routing vc allocator switch allocator traffic generators noc hardware. weeks 10-12: tiled multicore and noc, energy-efficient routers, qos mve341f4-p2"}, {"id": "MVE333DB-P3", "title": "Mini SIMT GPU from Scratch: ISA, Warp Scheduler, Divergence Stack and Kernels", "course": "MVE333DB", "course_title": "Multicore Computer Architecture", "sem": 3, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "12-18", "w0": 12, "total": 28, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 120, "hay": "mve333db-p3 mini simt gpu from scratch: isa, warp scheduler, divergence stack and kernels multicore computer architecture mve333db understanding gpus at microarchitecture level is rare. open projects exist (for example vortex, tine et al., micro 2021), so the value is the learning and the measured study, not novelty. design a small simt core: isa, assembler, warps, scoreboard, divergence handling, shared memory, simple memory coalescing; run vector-add, reduction and tiled matmul kernels; add performance counters and compare scaling trends qualitatively with cuda experiments from mve434p. isa + assembler fetch/decode warp scheduler lanes/alu divergence stack memory unit counters gpu microarchitecture. week 5: superscalar and gpu architectures; weeks 1-4 pipelines mve341f4-p3"}, {"id": "MVE333DC-P1", "title": "Elmore Delay Calculator for RC Trees Verified against ngspice", "course": "MVE333DC", "course_title": "VLSI Interconnect", "sem": 3, "track": "C", "diff": "Beginner", "label": "Build first", "wk": "3-4", "w0": 3, "total": 17, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 121, "hay": "mve333dc-p1 elmore delay calculator for rc trees verified against ngspice vlsi interconnect mve333dc elmore delay is the basis of many timing estimates. implement elmore and second-moment delay for rc trees in python; compare with ngspice transient for distributed lines; document accuracy limits. rc tree parser elmore spice compare interconnect modelling. weeks 1-2: elmore delay and rc trees mve232e3-p1"}, {"id": "MVE333DC-P2", "title": "Coupled-Line Crosstalk and Repeater Insertion Study with Extracted Parasitics", "course": "MVE333DC", "course_title": "VLSI Interconnect", "sem": 3, "track": "C", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 122, "hay": "mve333dc-p2 coupled-line crosstalk and repeater insertion study with extracted parasitics vlsi interconnect mve333dc spacing, shielding and repeaters trade delay, noise and area. extract parasitics from magic layouts of coupled lines; simulate delay/noise vs spacing and shielding; optimise repeaters analytically and by simulation. layouts extraction spice repeater optimiser signal integrity. weeks 6-8: crosstalk mitigation, coupled interconnects, capacitance extraction mve232e3-p2"}, {"id": "MVE333DC-P3", "title": "Wire-Energy-Aware Dataflow Study: Energy per Bit-mm in Systolic Arrays", "course": "MVE333DC", "course_title": "VLSI Interconnect", "sem": 3, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 23, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 123, "hay": "mve333dc-p3 wire-energy-aware dataflow study: energy per bit-mm in systolic arrays vlsi interconnect mve333dc data movement energy often dominates compute (see horowitz, isscc 2014). how does array size interact with wire energy? build a wire energy and delay model from extracted rc, apply it to systolic dataflows of various sizes, and identify where wire costs make larger arrays inefficient. wire model dataflow model scaling study physical-architecture thinking. weeks 1-5: scaling and power dissipation of interconnects mve232e3-p3"}, {"id": "MVE333DD-P1", "title": "HLS GEMM and FIR Kernels with Pragma Exploration", "course": "MVE333DD", "course_title": "C-based VLSI Design", "sem": 3, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 124, "hay": "mve333dd-p1 hls gemm and fir kernels with pragma exploration c-based vlsi design mve333dd hls performance depends on code structure and directives. write c++ kernels for fir and gemm, explore unrolling, pipelining and array partitioning in an hls tool, and tabulate latency/ii/resources. c++ kernels pragma sweep reports hls. weeks 3-7: hls flow, scheduling, binding, hardware-efficient c mve232e2-p1"}, {"id": "MVE333DD-P2", "title": "HLS vs Hand-Written RTL for a Conv Engine: PPA and Productivity", "course": "MVE333DD", "course_title": "C-based VLSI Design", "sem": 3, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 24, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 125, "hay": "mve333dd-p2 hls vs hand-written rtl for a conv engine: ppa and productivity c-based vlsi design mve333dd hls promises productivity; the cost in ppa must be measured. implement the same conv engine in hls and rtl, compare resources, fmax, latency and development effort (hours, lines). hls design rtl design comparison design methodology. weeks 5-9: binding, efficient c coding, compiler effects, verification mve232e2-p2"}, {"id": "MVE333DD-P3", "title": "Auto-Tuned HLS Design-Space Exploration with C/RTL Equivalence Checking", "course": "MVE333DD", "course_title": "C-based VLSI Design", "sem": 3, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "8-10", "w0": 8, "total": 25, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 126, "hay": "mve333dd-p3 auto-tuned hls design-space exploration with c/rtl equivalence checking c-based vlsi design mve333dd manual hls tuning is slow; search plus verification can automate it. build a search harness (bayesian or evolutionary) over pragma configurations with automatic c/rtl co-simulation to guard correctness; compare to random. config generator runner co-sim optimiser dse automation. weeks 9, 12: verification of hls, recent advances mve232e2-p3"}, {"id": "MVE434P-P1", "title": "INT8 Systolic GEMM Accelerator: AXI-Lite CSRs, DMA, Verified, with PPA", "course": "MVE434P", "course_title": "Minor Project", "sem": 3, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build first", "wk": "12-16", "w0": 12, "total": 27, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 127, "hay": "mve434p-p1 int8 systolic gemm accelerator: axi-lite csrs, dma, verified, with ppa minor project mve434p a complete accelerator block with a software-visible interface is the portfolio centrepiece; the gap is rigorous verification and ppa evidence. weight-stationary array with scratchpads, axi-lite csrs, axi-stream or dma data path, host driver, golden-model verification, coverage and openlane ppa. evaluate against a cpu baseline and the gemm scheduler. csr block dma scratchpads systolic array controller driver verification accelerator design, verification, pd. guideline: literature survey, gap analysis, in-house implementation (groups of two) mve433p-p1"}, {"id": "MVE434P-P2", "title": "GEMM Optimisation Ladder on GPU with Roofline: Naive to Tensor-Core-Aware", "course": "MVE434P", "course_title": "Minor Project", "sem": 3, "track": "B", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "10-14", "w0": 10, "total": 26, "cuda": true, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 128, "hay": "mve434p-p2 gemm optimisation ladder on gpu with roofline: naive to tensor-core-aware minor project mve434p gemm is the core workload; optimisation steps and profiler evidence show gpu architecture understanding. implement naive, coalesced, shared-memory tiled, register-blocked and (where supported) tensor-core wmma kernels; compare with cublas; roofline and counter analysis. kernels harness profiling roofline cuda optimisation. guideline: in-house implementation and analysis (groups of two) mve433p-p2"}, {"id": "MVE434P-P3", "title": "RV32 Core + Custom MAC Instruction + Small Cache: Measured CPI and Energy Proxy", "course": "MVE434P", "course_title": "Minor Project", "sem": 3, "track": "C", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "10-14", "w0": 10, "total": 25, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 129, "hay": "mve434p-p3 rv32 core + custom mac instruction + small cache: measured cpi and energy proxy minor project mve434p custom instructions and cache design show soc-level architecture skills. build on the rv32i core (mve315aa): add a dot-product/mac instruction, a direct-mapped i/d cache, and compiler intrinsics; run kernels and coremark; measure cpi, area and speedup. core custom unit caches intrinsics benchmarks cpu architecture. guideline: in-house implementation (groups of two) mve433p-p3"}, {"id": "MVE435N-P1", "title": "Verification-Focused Internship Plan: Coverage Closure on an Open IP Block", "course": "MVE435N", "course_title": "Industry Internship / Research Internship / Projects in CoEs", "sem": 3, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "6", "w0": 6, "total": 19, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 130, "hay": "mve435n-p1 verification-focused internship plan: coverage closure on an open ip block industry internship / research internship / projects in coes mve435n verification hiring is large; a concrete coverage and bug-finding record is persuasive. pick an open ip block (for example from a mainstream open-source risc-v or peripheral repository), build or extend a testbench, close defined coverage goals, file reproducible issues. if the host is a company, adapt to their ip under nda rules. plan testbench coverage issue log verification practice. internship guideline: work related to the specialisation at industry, a research organisation or an rvce coe mve434n-p1"}, {"id": "MVE435N-P2", "title": "PPA-Improvement Internship Plan on an Open Design", "course": "MVE435N", "course_title": "Industry Internship / Research Internship / Projects in CoEs", "sem": 3, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "6", "w0": 6, "total": 24, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 131, "hay": "mve435n-p2 ppa-improvement internship plan on an open design industry internship / research internship / projects in coes mve435n physical-design roles value measured ppa improvements with discipline. take an open design through a documented flow, identify bottlenecks, apply targeted fixes, and report before/after ppa; with company tools if host provides. baseline analysis changes report physical design. internship guideline mve434n-p2"}, {"id": "MVE435N-P3", "title": "GPU/Firmware Performance-Engineering Internship Plan", "course": "MVE435N", "course_title": "Industry Internship / Research Internship / Projects in CoEs", "sem": 3, "track": "B", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "6", "w0": 6, "total": 21, "cuda": true, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 132, "hay": "mve435n-p3 gpu/firmware performance-engineering internship plan industry internship / research internship / projects in coes mve435n performance and bring-up skills are a distinct career path. profile a workload (cuda, cpu or firmware), identify bottlenecks, optimise, and document with before/after measurements; or perform a firmware bring-up task with documented tests. baseline profile optimise report performance engineering. internship guideline mve434n-p3"}, {"id": "MVE341EA-P1", "title": "One-Command Reproducible RTL-to-GDS Flow with Sign-Off Checklist", "course": "MVE341EA", "course_title": "VLSI Design Flow: RTL to GDS", "sem": 4, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 22, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-4.html", "ord": 133, "hay": "mve341ea-p1 one-command reproducible rtl-to-gds flow with sign-off checklist vlsi design flow: rtl to gds mve341ea reproducibility is what makes ppa claims credible. wrap the open flow in a makefile or workflow with pinned tool versions, containerisation, automated sign-off checks (drc, lvs, sta, lec) and archived reports for two designs. container flow wrapper checks reports flow engineering. weeks 2-12: the complete flow mve341f1-p1"}, {"id": "MVE341EA-P2", "title": "Tape-Out Readiness Dashboard and Regression Across Designs", "course": "MVE341EA", "course_title": "VLSI Design Flow: RTL to GDS", "sem": 4, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 19, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-4.html", "ord": 134, "hay": "mve341ea-p2 tape-out readiness dashboard and regression across designs vlsi design flow: rtl to gds mve341ea teams track dozens of metrics across blocks; automation reveals regressions. collect metrics from multiple designs and runs, build a dashboard with thresholds, trends, and failure drill-down. collectors db dashboard tooling. weeks 7-12: sta, constraints, physical verification and sign-off mve341f1-p2"}, {"id": "MVE341EA-P3", "title": "Flow Hyperparameter Tuning with Bayesian Optimisation vs DoE", "course": "MVE341EA", "course_title": "VLSI Design Flow: RTL to GDS", "sem": 4, "track": "C", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "8-10", "w0": 8, "total": 25, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-4.html", "ord": 135, "hay": "mve341ea-p3 flow hyperparameter tuning with bayesian optimisation vs doe vlsi design flow: rtl to gds mve341ea flow parameters strongly affect ppa; openroad already offers an autotuner, so the contribution is a controlled comparison. compare random search, doe (from mim426rt), openroad autotuner and your bo on multiple designs with seeds; report results with statistics. search space runner optimisers stats eda optimisation. weeks 4-12: optimisation parameters across the flow mve341f1-p3"}, {"id": "MVE341EB-P1", "title": "SAR ADC Behavioural Model with Capacitor Mismatch and ENOB Analysis", "course": "MVE341EB", "course_title": "VLSI Data Conversion Circuits", "sem": 4, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-4.html", "ord": 136, "hay": "mve341eb-p1 sar adc behavioural model with capacitor mismatch and enob analysis vlsi data conversion circuits mve341eb enob depends on mismatch, noise and comparator offset. model a sar adc in python with capacitor mismatch and comparator noise, compute inl/dnl/enob, and implement the sar logic in rtl. behavioural model metrics sar rtl data converters. weeks 3 and 5: fft-based characterisation and adc metrics (the sar architecture itself is in mve221ia unit iv) mve341f3-p1"}, {"id": "MVE341EB-P2", "title": "Sigma-Delta Modulator Study with CIC Decimator in RTL", "course": "MVE341EB", "course_title": "VLSI Data Conversion Circuits", "sem": 4, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-4.html", "ord": 137, "hay": "mve341eb-p2 sigma-delta modulator study with cic decimator in rtl vlsi data conversion circuits mve341eb oversampling trades speed for resolution; noise shaping needs decimation hardware. simulate first/second-order modulators, compute snr vs osr, implement cic decimation in rtl and validate end-to-end. modulator model cic snr analysis mixed-signal dsp. weeks 9-11: oversampling, discrete- and continuous-time delta-sigma modulators mve341f3-p2"}, {"id": "MVE341EB-P3", "title": "ADC Resolution vs Accuracy and Energy in Compute-in-Memory Readout (System Study)", "course": "MVE341EB", "course_title": "VLSI Data Conversion Circuits", "sem": 4, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "8-10", "w0": 8, "total": 22, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-4.html", "ord": 138, "hay": "mve341eb-p3 adc resolution vs accuracy and energy in compute-in-memory readout (system study) vlsi data conversion circuits mve341eb analog compute-in-memory moves cost to adcs. the question is how adc bits trade accuracy, energy and throughput. model crossbar column sums with noise and variation, quantise with adc models of various resolutions, evaluate network accuracy and adc energy using published figure-of-merit assumptions (for example murmann's adc survey as a labelled source); simulation-only. crossbar model adc model accuracy evaluation energy model analog-digital system analysis. weeks 1-7: sampling, adc metrics and flash architecture applied to analog dot-product readout mve341f3-p3"}, {"id": "MVE341EC-P1", "title": "Logical-Effort Path Sizing Tool Calibrated and Verified Against ngspice in sky130", "course": "MVE341EC", "course_title": "CMOS Digital VLSI Design", "sem": 4, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "3-5", "w0": 3, "total": 22, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-4.html", "ord": 139, "hay": "mve341ec-p1 logical-effort path sizing tool calibrated and verified against ngspice in sky130 cmos digital vlsi design mve341ec logical effort gives a quick estimate of the best gate sizes and number of stages, but its parameters must be calibrated for a real process. the project checks how good those estimates are in sky130. characterise sky130 inverter, nand2, nor2 and a complex gate in ngspice to extract tau, parasitic delay p and logical effort g. build a tool that, for a given path and load, computes the optimal number of stages and gate sizes, generates a sized spice netlist and compares predicted with simulated delay on decoders, a carry chain and a high-fanout buffer chain. gate characterisation parameter extraction (tau, p, g) path optimiser netlist generator spice verification error analysis logical effort, gate characterisation, spice automation. weeks 3-5: combinational logic design and logical effort; weeks 1-2: inverter, power and spice simulation "}, {"id": "MVE341EC-P2", "title": "Mini Standard-Cell Library: Layout, Characterisation to Liberty and OpenROAD Use", "course": "MVE341EC", "course_title": "CMOS Digital VLSI Design", "sem": 4, "track": "C", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "6-9", "w0": 6, "total": 23, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-4.html", "ord": 140, "hay": "mve341ec-p2 mini standard-cell library: layout, characterisation to liberty and openroad use cmos digital vlsi design mve341ec standard cell libraries tie transistor-level design to the timing models synthesis uses. building a few cells and characterising them makes the weeks on inverters, combinational and sequential cells concrete. lay out inv, nand2, nor2, aoi21 and a dff; script ngspice characterisation to liberty tables, generate lef; run a small design through openroad with your cells. layouts characterisation scripts liberty lef flow test library flow. weeks 2-7: cmos inverter, spice simulation, combinational and sequential cell design mve427sl-p2"}, {"id": "MVE341EC-P3", "title": "6T SRAM Bitcell and Sense Amplifier in sky130: Read/Write Margins and Monte-Carlo Yield", "course": "MVE341EC", "course_title": "CMOS Digital VLSI Design", "sem": 4, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 25, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-4.html", "ord": 141, "hay": "mve341ec-p3 6t sram bitcell and sense amplifier in sky130: read/write margins and monte-carlo yield cmos digital vlsi design mve341ec on-chip sram dominates accelerator area and much of its energy. the bitcell must read without flipping and write reliably under variation, and those margins shrink at low voltage. design a 6t bitcell, a small column with precharge and a latch-type sense amplifier in sky130. measure hold and read static noise margin (butterfly curves), write margin, read current and access time vs supply voltage. run monte-carlo on threshold mismatch to estimate the failure probability per cell, and relate it to the yield of an accelerator scratchpad of a given size. 6t bitcell butterfly snm analysis write-margin test column with precharge latch sense amplifier monte-carlo yield sram design, noise-margin analysis, statistical yield estimation. week 8: memory design; weeks 5-7: sequential logic and clocking (sense-amplifier timing); weeks 1-2: device models "}, {"id": "MVE341ED-P1", "title": "Type-II Charge-Pump PLL Behavioural Model and Loop-Filter Design", "course": "MVE341ED", "course_title": "Phase-Locked Loops", "sem": 4, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 18, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-4.html", "ord": 142, "hay": "mve341ed-p1 type-ii charge-pump pll behavioural model and loop-filter design phase-locked loops mve341ed loop filter design sets bandwidth, phase margin and lock time. build a python/verilog-ams-free behavioural model and a discrete-time simulation; design loop filter for phase margin; compare with simulation lock time. model loop filter design simulation pll design. weeks 1-5: pll analysis and charge-pump design procedure mve341f2-p1"}, {"id": "MVE341ED-P2", "title": "All-Digital PLL in RTL (TDC, Loop Filter, DCO Model)", "course": "MVE341ED", "course_title": "Phase-Locked Loops", "sem": 4, "track": "C", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-4.html", "ord": 143, "hay": "mve341ed-p2 all-digital pll in rtl (tdc, loop filter, dco model) phase-locked loops mve341ed digital plls are synthesizable and scalable. implement tdc, digital loop filter, dco behavioural model; verify lock, jitter and tracking via simulation. tdc filter dco model jitter analysis digital clocking. weeks 11-12: digital plls mve341f2-p2"}, {"id": "MVE341ED-P3", "title": "PLL Jitter Budget and Its Effect on MAC-Array Fmax", "course": "MVE341ED", "course_title": "Phase-Locked Loops", "sem": 4, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "7-9", "w0": 7, "total": 22, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-4.html", "ord": 144, "hay": "mve341ed-p3 pll jitter budget and its effect on mac-array fmax phase-locked loops mve341ed jitter consumes timing margin; the question is how much frequency is lost. characterise ring-oscillator jitter in spice, convert to uncertainty, apply in sta constraints for the mac array, and quantify fmax loss vs jitter. jitter extraction sta constraint link fmax study cross-domain analysis. weeks 5-9: noise analysis, ring oscillators, phase noise mve341f2-p3"}, {"id": "MVE442PA-P1", "title": "Quantised Inference SoC: RISC-V Host + INT8 Systolic Engine + DMA, FPGA and PPA", "course": "MVE442PA", "course_title": "Major Project", "sem": 4, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Build first", "wk": "18", "w0": 18, "total": 27, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-4.html", "ord": 145, "hay": "mve442pa-p1 quantised inference soc: risc-v host + int8 systolic engine + dma, fpga and ppa major project mve442pa integration is the hard part: host, driver, dma, accelerator and memory must work together and be measured against baselines. integrate your rv32 core, the minor-project accelerator, dma and scratchpads over axi; software stack with driver and a compiler-like layer mapping a small cnn or transformer block; end-to-end inference, measured against cpu and gpu baselines. risc-v host axi interconnect dma accelerator driver model mapper benchmarks soc integration, hw/sw co-design. guideline: gap analysis, individual implementation, mandatory presentation or publication mve442p-p1"}, {"id": "MVE442PA-P2", "title": "Mixed-Precision Sparse Dataflow Accelerator with Analytical Model Validated Against RTL", "course": "MVE442PA", "course_title": "Major Project", "sem": 4, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Build next", "wk": "18", "w0": 18, "total": 28, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-4.html", "ord": 146, "hay": "mve442pa-p2 mixed-precision sparse dataflow accelerator with analytical model validated against rtl major project mve442pa architecture exploration needs fast models validated against rtl; the claim is model accuracy and design guidance. implement int8/int4 and structured sparsity support in rtl; build a timeloop-style analytical model; validate cycle counts and energy trends vs rtl; explore dataflows; related published work is extensive (for example eyeriss, eie, scnn, timeloop) so define your gap precisely. precision-scalable pe sparse controller analytical model rtl validation dse architecture research. guideline: research gap analysis, mandatory presentation or publication mve442p-p2"}, {"id": "MVE442PA-P3", "title": "Open SIMT GPU with CUDA-Like Toolchain: Assembler, Runtime and Performance Counters", "course": "MVE442PA", "course_title": "Major Project", "sem": 4, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "18", "w0": 18, "total": 26, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-4.html", "ord": 147, "hay": "mve442pa-p3 open simt gpu with cuda-like toolchain: assembler, runtime and performance counters major project mve442pa a vertically integrated mini-gpu stack shows microarchitecture, compiler and runtime/firmware understanding. extend the simt core (mve333db) with a small c-subset-to-isa compiler front-end or ptx-like assembler, a host runtime/driver (launch, memory copy, completion), and counters; run gemm and reduction kernels and analyse scaling; compare qualitatively with cuda experiments. simt core compiler runtime/driver host interface counters kernels gpu systems stack. guideline: individual implementation, mandatory presentation or publication mve442p-p3"}]};